WN.lb(9,{"mt_82KKv0Fca3":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which word means how formal or chatty writing is?","choices":["register","tone","form"],"answer":0,"hint":"One word is about the feeling, one about the kind of text, and one about formality.","explain":"Register is how formal the writing is. Tone is the feeling, and form is the kind of text."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"\"Don't miss the fair: it is the best day of the year!\" What is the purpose?","choices":["to persuade","to inform","to entertain"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does it give facts, tell a story, or try to get you to do something?","explain":"It gives an opinion and tells you what to do, so its purpose is to persuade."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which sentence is evidence?","choices":["8 out of 10 children said the field was muddy.","The field is the worst place ever.","Is the field muddy?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Evidence is something you could check.","explain":"A survey result can be checked, so it is evidence. The others are an opinion and a question."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A letter, a poster and a poem are different…","choices":["forms","tones","registers"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the kind of text, not the feeling.","explain":"Letter, poster and poem are kinds of text, which we call forms."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Leo writes \"Then\" to start every sentence. What is the problem?","choices":["then only shows time order, not reasons or contrasts","then is a rude word","there is no problem"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does then tell the reader about how two ideas link?","explain":"Then shows time order. Cohesive writing uses the word that fits the link: because, however, for example."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"\"I crept into the cave and held my breath.\" Which perspective is this?","choices":["first person","third person","second person"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the word I. Who is telling the story?","explain":"I means the narrator is in the story, so it is first person."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Kai writes to the council about {n} broken swings in the park. Which sentence fits best?","choices":["I am writing to report that {n} swings in the park are broken.","Yo, {n} swings are bust!","{n} swings. Broken. Sort it."],"answer":0,"hint":"The council is a stranger in a public job. How formal should Kai be?","explain":"A letter to the council needs formal register: full words, no slang, and a clear, polite report of the {n} broken swings.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why does a writer ask \"Do you want slugs eating your lettuces?\"","choices":["to make the reader think and agree","because the writer does not know","to give a fact"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the writer expect an answer?","explain":"It is a rhetorical question: the writer does not want an answer, just for you to think no, and agree."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"A poster says: \"{p} out of 10 pupils want a longer break. Join us!\" Which part is evidence?","choices":["{p} out of 10 pupils want a longer break","Join us!","none of it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Evidence is a fact or number you could check.","explain":"The survey result, {p} out of 10 pupils, can be checked, so it is evidence. Join us! is an order that tries to persuade.","params":{"p":[6,9]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Ava writes to her head teacher to ask for {n} extra days on her project. Which opening fits best?","choices":["Dear Mrs Shah, I am writing to ask…","Hiya! Quick one…","Oi, can I…"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who is the reader? How formal should it be?","explain":"A head teacher needs formal writing, even for a short request of {n} days: Dear…, full words, polite.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"\"It was cold. ____, we went for a swim.\" Which word fits?","choices":["However","Because","For example"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the swim go with the cold, or against it?","explain":"Swimming goes against what you expect on a cold day, so you need a contrast word: however."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"An advert has {q} rhetorical questions and {d} other sentences. How many sentences does it have in all?","answer":"q+d","hint":"A rhetorical question is still a sentence. Add them all up.","explain":"{q} questions and {d} other sentences make {=q+d} sentences. Spotting how many questions a writer uses helps you see the technique.","params":{"q":[1,4],"d":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"\"Thanks a LOT for stepping on my sandcastle.\" What is the tone?","choices":["sarcastic","grateful","formal"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it good news when someone steps on your sandcastle?","explain":"The words say thanks, but the writer means the opposite. That is a sarcastic tone."}],"explain":{"kid":["Writers pick their words on purpose. Craft words help us talk about those picks.","Register is how formal your writing is. To a friend you can say Hey! To a head teacher you write Dear Mr Ali. Who reads it decides.","Purpose is why you write. You can tell facts, make someone agree, or tell a story. Form is the kind of text, like a letter or a poem. Tone is the feeling, like cross or kind.","Cohesion means your sentences link up. Words like however, because and for example show how two ideas fit. A rhetorical question makes the reader think. Evidence is a fact you can check."],"adult":["From about Year 5 the English curriculum expects children to talk about their writing with technical words: purpose, audience, form, register, cohesion, and devices such as rhetorical questions. The point is not to memorise labels. It is to see that each feature is a choice, made for a reader, and to explain its effect.","Register is the formality of the language, set by the reader and the situation. Tone is the attitude or feeling, which can be warm, angry or sarcastic at any level of formality. Form is the type of text, such as a letter, a report or a poem. Cohesion is the linking between sentences, through connectives, pronouns and repeated words; coherence is whether the whole piece makes sense in a sensible order.","Common mistakes: mixing up tone and register; thinking formal just means long words, rather than full forms, no slang and polite openings; treating any linking word as correct, so then is used where however or because is needed; calling an opinion evidence; and naming a technique without saying what it does to the reader.","At home, rewrite one message together for two readers, such as a text to a cousin and an email to a teacher, and talk about what changed and why. When reading adverts or news, ask what the writer wants you to think and which words do the work."]},"familyTip":"Write one message twice with your child: a text to a friend and a note to a teacher. Ask which words changed, and why the reader matters.","keyIdea":"Writers make choices on purpose, and craft words name those choices. Register is how formal or chatty the writing is, and the reader decides it. Purpose is why you write: to inform, persuade or entertain. Form is the kind of text, tone is the feeling, and cohesion is how the sentences link. Techniques such as rhetorical questions and evidence are tools to affect the reader.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Formal or informal? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Formal","Informal"],"items":[["Dear Sir or Madam",0],["Hiya!",1],["I would be grateful if…",0],["gonna",1],["Yours sincerely",0],["See ya!",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcraft","caption":"Flagship lab: slide the register dial for each reader, sort openings by purpose, link a paragraph and label techniques.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_o8ciHks8t2":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is a draft?","choices":["Your first go at writing it down","Fixing the spelling","Drawing a picture"],"answer":0,"hint":"It comes after the plan, and it can be messy.","explain":"A draft is the first go. It can be messy, and you make it better later."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Leo's story has {n} sentences with no full stops. What should he do?","choices":["Edit it","Plan it again","Throw it away"],"answer":0,"hint":"Full stops are mistakes you fix.","explain":"Adding the {n} missing full stops is editing.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which change is revising?","choices":["Adding \"shiny\" to the red car","Fixing a capital letter","Fixing a spelling"],"answer":0,"hint":"Revising makes the words and ideas better.","explain":"Adding a detail word makes it better, so that is revising. The others are editing."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What is the purpose of writing?","choices":["Who reads it","Why you write it","How long it is"],"answer":1,"hint":"Purpose answers the question why.","explain":"The purpose is why you write it. The audience is who reads it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"\"On Saturday I went to the park with Mum.\" What kind of writing is this?","choices":["A recount","Instructions","A made-up story"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did it really happen to the writer?","explain":"It tells what really happened, so it is a recount."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Sam's plan has {n} ideas. He puts them in order: first, next, last. What is that order called?","choices":["The audience","The sequence","The draft"],"answer":1,"hint":"It is the order things happen in.","explain":"Putting the {n} ideas in order makes a sequence.","params":{"n":[3,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"\"Once upon a time, a cat could fly.\" What kind of writing is this?","choices":["A recount","Instructions","A story"],"answer":2,"hint":"Ask yourself: can a cat really fly?","explain":"It is made up, so it is a story, also called a narrative."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Mia wrote her draft. Is her writing finished?","choices":["Yes, a draft is done","No, she can revise and edit it","Yes, if it is long"],"answer":1,"hint":"Writing has more steps after the draft.","explain":"After the draft she revises to make it better and edits to fix mistakes."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What do writers do first?","choices":["Plan","Edit","Revise"],"answer":0,"hint":"You think of ideas before you write.","explain":"First you plan: think of ideas and put them in order."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Ava adds {n} detail words to make her writing better. What is she doing?","choices":["Editing","Revising","Planning"],"answer":1,"hint":"Making the words and ideas better has a special name.","explain":"Adding {n} detail words makes it better, so Ava is revising.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Tom finds {n} spelling slips and fixes them. What is he doing?","choices":["Editing","Planning","Drafting"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fixing mistakes is one step.","explain":"Fixing {n} spelling slips is editing, also called proofreading.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"\"First, get a cup. Next, pour the milk.\" What kind of writing is this?","choices":["A story","Instructions","A recount"],"answer":1,"hint":"Does it tell you how to do something?","explain":"It tells you how to do it in steps, so it is instructions."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Kim writes a thank-you card to Gran. Who is the audience?","choices":["Kim","Gran","The teacher"],"answer":1,"hint":"The audience is who will read it.","explain":"Gran will read the card, so Gran is the audience."}],"explain":{"kid":["Writers do not write it all in one go. They do steps. First they plan. A plan is the ideas you think of. You put them in order: first, next, last. That order is the sequence.","Then they draft. A draft is your first go. You write it down. It can be messy. That is OK.","Next they revise. Revise means make it better. You can add a detail word, like big or fluffy. Then they edit. Edit means fix mistakes. Look for a capital letter, a full stop and the spelling.","Ask: who will read it? That is the audience. Ask: why am I writing? That is the purpose. Instructions tell how to do a thing. A recount tells what you did. A story is made up."],"adult":["This topic gives children aged 5 to 8 the words for the steps every writer takes: compose, plan, draft, revise, edit and proofread, plus audience, purpose, sequence, detail, paragraph and three kinds (genres) of writing: instructions, recount and narrative. Knowing the names helps children see writing as a process, not one sitting.","The lab is all taps with big targets. Children put plan, draft, revise and edit in order, plan a three-part kite story, add detail words to a draft and watch the picture change, tap three mistakes to edit a sentence, and match writing jobs to instructions, a recount or a story.","Common mistakes: mixing up revising and editing. Revising changes the ideas and words to make the writing better; editing fixes surface errors such as capitals, full stops and spelling. Many children also think the first draft must be neat, or that writing is finished once the last word is on the page. Some confuse a recount, which really happened, with a story, which is made up.","At home, when your child writes a card or list, use the words: shall we plan first? This is your draft. Can we revise it with one more detail? Now let's edit for capitals and full stops. Ask who the audience is before they start."]},"familyTip":"Write a birthday card together in four steps: plan what to say, draft it on scrap paper, revise by adding one detail word, then edit for a capital letter and full stop before copying it neatly.","keyIdea":"Writers work in steps, and each step has a name. Plan means think of ideas and put them in order, the sequence. Draft means write it down, even if it is messy. Revise means make the words and ideas better, for example by adding detail. Edit, or proofread, means fix mistakes such as capitals, full stops and spelling. The audience is who will read it and the purpose is why you write it, which decides the kind: instructions tell how, a recount tells what really happened, and a story, or narrative, is made up.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Revise or edit? Ask: does it make it better, or fix a mistake?","opts":{"bins":["Revise","Edit"],"items":[["Add a detail word",0],["Change a dull word",0],["Add a new idea",0],["Fix a capital letter",1],["Add a full stop",1],["Fix a spelling",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagwproc","caption":"Flagship lab: put the writing steps in order, plan a story, revise with detail words, edit three mistakes and match each job to its kind of writing.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_j6ENpc8--_":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is a mentor text?","choices":["A book you copy word for word","A text you study to borrow an author's methods","A list of spelling rules"],"answer":1,"hint":"Think of a mentor as someone you learn from.","explain":"A mentor text is one you study to learn how an author did something, such as show a character, then use that method in your own words."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which plan note shows that Lola is shy?","choices":["Lola is shy.","Lola is very, very shy.","Lola hides behind her book when the teacher asks a question."],"answer":2,"hint":"Look for something the reader could picture.","explain":"Hiding behind a book is an action that shows shyness. The other notes tell the trait, and adding very still tells."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A character says, “I'm fine,” but thinks, “I want to go home.” What does the gap show?","choices":["That the character is hiding a feeling","That the character is lying for fun","Nothing, because speech always tells the truth"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare what is said with what is thought.","explain":"When speech and thought do not match, the reader sees a hidden feeling. Authors use this gap to show, not tell."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which method is used here: “Her coat was two sizes too big and patched at the elbows”?","choices":["Speech","Looks","Thought"],"answer":1,"hint":"Is this something she says, thinks, or how she appears?","explain":"It describes how she appears, so the method is looks. It hints that her family may not have much money."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Your character waits {n} minutes at the bus stop. Which note best shows he is bored?","choices":["He was bored.","He counts the cracks in the pavement, then counts them again.","He is very bored."],"answer":1,"hint":"Pick what he does, not a feeling word.","explain":"Counting cracks twice is an action that shows boredom. After {n} minutes the reader can feel it too, without the word bored.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"You loved how a mentor text zooms in on a spider. How should you use it?","choices":["Copy the spider sentence word for word","Zoom in on a different detail in your own setting","Skip it, because borrowing is cheating"],"answer":1,"hint":"Borrow the method, not the words.","explain":"Copying the sentence is not your writing. Borrowing the zoom, with your own details, is how writers learn from mentor texts."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which setting detail builds a calm, safe mood?","choices":["A warm fire crackles while rain taps on the window.","Footsteps creak on the stairs above.","The lights flicker and go out."],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one would make you feel cosy?","explain":"A warm fire with rain outside feels safe and calm. The other two make the reader uneasy."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Your plan says only: 1. Jay goes to camp. 2. He gets lost. 3. He is found. What is missing?","choices":["Nothing, a plan is just the plot","Notes on how to show Jay and the setting's mood","Ten more events"],"answer":1,"hint":"A plan is more than what happens.","explain":"A list of events is a plot, not a full plan. Add notes on how Jay acts, speaks and thinks, and setting details for the mood you want."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"A setting has {n} details. Which order builds suspense like a camera zoom?","choices":["Start close, then go wide","Start wide, then move closer each time","Put the details in any order"],"answer":1,"hint":"Picture a camera moving in.","explain":"Start wide so the reader knows where they are, then move closer with each detail. The close-up at the end makes the reader lean in.","params":{"n":[2,9]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which line shows that Ben is angry, instead of telling us?","choices":["Ben was really angry.","Ben slammed his bag on the table and said nothing.","Ben felt anger inside him."],"answer":1,"hint":"Look for an action the reader can picture, with no feeling word.","explain":"Slamming the bag is an action. The reader works out that Ben is angry, so the line shows it. The other two name the feeling."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"In a mentor text, Mia checks the clock {n} times in one minute. What is the author showing?","choices":["That Mia is calm and relaxed","That the clock is broken","That Mia is worried or impatient"],"answer":2,"hint":"Why would someone keep looking at a clock?","explain":"Checking the clock {n} times is an action that shows worry or impatience. The author never names the feeling, so the reader works it out.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"You want an uneasy mood outside an old house. Which detail fits best?","choices":["A door creaks open, but no one is there.","Birds sing in the sunny garden.","The house was very spooky."],"answer":0,"hint":"Pick a detail you can hear or see, not a word that names the mood.","explain":"A creaking door with no one there makes the reader uneasy. Birds singing fights the mood, and very spooky just tells it."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Your setting plan has {n} creepy details and one cheerful one. What should you do?","choices":["Add more cheerful details to balance it","Cut or change the cheerful detail so every note builds the same mood","Keep it, because more details are always better"],"answer":1,"hint":"What is the job of each setting detail?","explain":"Every detail should build the one mood you chose. One cheerful note among {n} creepy ones breaks the spell, so cut it or change it.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A mentor text zooms in. Which order goes from wide to close-up?","choices":["A tear on her cheek, her face, the crowded hall","Her face, the crowded hall, a tear on her cheek","The crowded hall, her face, a tear on her cheek"],"answer":2,"hint":"Start with the biggest view and end with the smallest detail.","explain":"A camera zoom starts wide, the crowded hall, then moves to her face, then to one tear. Each step is closer than the last."}],"explain":{"kid":["Good writers read like writers. They pick a book they love and ask: how did the author do that? That book is a mentor text. You borrow the method, not the words.","One method is show, don't tell. Telling names the feeling: Sam was nervous. Showing lets the reader work it out: Sam folded the note, then unfolded it. Plan what your character does, says and thinks. Do not plan the word nervous.","A setting is more than where the story happens. It sets a mood. A gate that creaks and a window that goes dark make us uneasy. Bright sun and kids on swings make us calm. Pick details that all build the same mood.","Some authors zoom in like a camera. They start wide, with the whole valley. Then the lane. Then the door. Then one spider on the knocker. Each line gets closer, and the reader leans in. Put these notes in your plan before you write."],"adult":["This topic is about planning a story before drafting, using a mentor text as a model. Learners study how a published author reveals a character or builds a setting, then use the same method in a plan of their own. The aim is to copy the craft, not the sentences.","In the lab, learners sort lines into telling and showing and label a short mentor paragraph by method: action, speech, thought or looks. They then plan a new character through three notes and choose setting details that build an uneasy mood. Last, they order lines from a wide view to a close-up.","Common mistakes: naming the trait in the plan, such as brave, so the draft just tells; adding very instead of a detail; mixing cheerful and creepy details so the mood is lost; and copying the mentor's sentences instead of its method. A plan made only of plot events, with no notes on character or setting, is another mistake to watch for.","Check the plan, not just the story. Each character note should be something seen or heard, and each setting note should fit the chosen mood. Learners at this age often find it easier to plan in short notes than in full sentences."]},"familyTip":"Read the first page of a favourite book together. Ask: how do we know what this character is like, without being told? Then plan one new character using the same trick.","keyIdea":"Plan a story by borrowing methods from a mentor text, not its words. Show a character through action, speech and thought instead of naming the trait, choose setting details that build one mood, and move from a wide view to a close-up to build suspense.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Plan notes: does each one show it or tell it? Drag each note, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Shows it","Tells it"],"items":[["She chews her pencil and stares at the clock",0],["Rain drums on the tin roof",0],["“Go on, then,” he said, not looking up",0],["She was worried",1],["The place felt scary",1],["He was very kind",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagmentor","caption":"Flagship lab: sort telling from showing, read a mentor text like a writer, plan a character and a setting mood, and borrow the author's zoom.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_OyOYHlZ2_T":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is the best way to check your writing makes sense?","choices":["Read it out loud, pointing at each word","Look at it quickly","Count the words"],"answer":0,"hint":"How can you hear a missing word?","explain":"Read it out loud and point at each word. Then you read what you wrote, not what you meant."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Tom wrote: 'I have {n} cats.' The question is: 'What do your cats look like?' Which detail fits?","choices":["I have {n} fat, ginger cats.","I have {n} cats at home.","I have had {n} cats since Monday."],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for describing words.","explain":"'Fat' and 'ginger' are describing words (adjectives). They tell what the {n} cats look like.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which joining word adds a reason?","choices":["because","and","then"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word answers 'why?'","explain":"'Because' joins a reason to a sentence: 'I wore a hat because it was sunny.'"},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Your friend says: 'I like your word splash.' Is that a star or a wish?","choices":["A star","A wish","Neither"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does it say what is good, or ask for a change?","explain":"A star. It tells you what is good, so keep doing it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Lily wrote: 'We played {n} games.' Her teacher asks: 'Who did you play with?' Which change fits?","choices":["We played {n} games with Nan.","We played {n} games in the hall.","We played {n} fun games."],"answer":0,"hint":"The question is 'who?'. Find a person.","explain":"'With Nan' tells who. 'In the hall' tells where, and 'fun' tells what the games were like.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"You add a word to your sentence. What should you do next?","choices":["Read the sentence again","Start a new story","Rub out the first word"],"answer":0,"hint":"A change can make a new slip.","explain":"Read it again. A new word can land in the wrong place, like 'a cat cat'."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"The teacher asks: 'Can you tell me more about the cake?' What should you write?","choices":["A detail, like 'a big chocolate cake'","The words 'Can you tell me more?'","Nothing, it is finished"],"answer":0,"hint":"Answer the question. Do not copy it.","explain":"Add a detail that answers it, like 'a big chocolate cake'. Do not copy the question into your writing."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Sam wrote: 'I like to my scooter.' Read it out. What is wrong?","choices":["A word is missing, like 'ride'","Nothing, it is fine","It needs a full stop in the middle"],"answer":0,"hint":"Point at each word as you read it. Does it sound right?","explain":"A word is missing. 'I like to ride my scooter.' Reading it out loud helps you hear the gap."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Mia wrote: 'I saw {n} birds.' Her teacher asks: 'Where were they?' Which change answers the question?","choices":["I saw {n} birds in a tree.","I saw {n} birds on Monday.","I saw {n} little birds."],"answer":0,"hint":"The question is 'where?'. Find the words that tell a place.","explain":"'In a tree' tells where. 'On Monday' tells when and 'little' tells what they looked like.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Ben wrote: 'I was sad.' His friend asks: 'Why?' Which is the best change?","choices":["I was sad because my toy broke.","I was sad because I was sad.","I was sad and then."],"answer":0,"hint":"A reason tells what made it happen. Use 'because'.","explain":"'Because my toy broke' gives a reason. 'Because I was sad' just says the same thing again."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Your story has {n} sentences. Your teacher gives you two stars and a wish. What is the wish?","choices":["A job to make your writing better","A sign your writing is bad","Something to rub out"],"answer":0,"hint":"Stars say what is good. What does a wish ask you to do?","explain":"A wish is a job to do. It helps all {n} sentences get better. It is not a telling off.","params":{"n":[3,8]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Read your writing out loud. Does it sound right? If not, a word may be missing.","A reader may ask you a question. What did it look like? Where were you? Add words that answer that question. Big and spotty tell what a dog looks like. By the river tells where.","If the reader asks why, add a reason. Use the joining word because.","A star says what is good. A wish is a job to do. It does not mean your work is bad. After you change it, read it again."],"adult":["This is the start of revising, and children need an adult to guide it. The English National Curriculum asks Year 1 pupils to re-read what they have written to check that it makes sense and to discuss what they have written with the teacher or other pupils. In Year 2 they make simple additions, revisions and corrections. The US Common Core standard W.1.5 says the same in its own words: respond to questions and suggestions from peers and add details to strengthen writing.","Two skills sit at the heart of it. First, re-reading aloud: children often 'read' what they meant rather than what they wrote, so a missing word goes unnoticed until they point at each word as they say it. Second, matching a detail to the question. A common mistake is to add any detail at all. Asked 'What did the dog look like?', a child adds 'at the park', which is a good detail but answers 'where?'.","Use the proper words with your child: sentence, re-read, detail, describing word (adjective), joining word (conjunction) and 'because'. A noun with describing words in front, such as 'a big, spotty dog', is what Year 2 calls an expanded noun phrase. Two stars and a wish is a common classroom format, and many children at first hear a wish as criticism. Say plainly that a wish is a job, and that every writer gets them.","In the lab your child drops a missing word into the right gap and hears what a wrong gap sounds like, answers a reader's questions with details that fit, adds a reason with 'because', sorts stars from wishes, and taps each new detail in a before-and-after story."]},"familyTip":"After your child writes or tells you a sentence, ask one question about it: 'What colour was it?' or 'Why?' Let them add the answer, then read the new sentence together, pointing at each word.","keyIdea":"Good writers re-read their work out loud to check that it makes sense. When a reader asks a question, such as 'What did it look like?' or 'Why?', add a detail that answers that question, or a reason with 'because'. A star says what is good. A wish is a job to do, not a telling off. After each change, read the sentence again.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"The question was 'What did the dog look like?' Does the detail answer it? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["✓ Answers the question","✗ Answers a different question"],"items":[["big and spotty",0],["with a long, waggy tail",0],["tiny and brown",0],["fluffy and white",0],["at the park",1],["on Monday",1],["because it was hungry",1],["with my brother",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagfback","caption":"Flagship lab: read back and add the missing word, answer a reader's questions with details, add a because reason, sort stars and wishes, spot new details.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_4A4RpX-Go9":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What does research mean?","choices":["Finding out facts to answer a question","Making up a story","Copying a whole book"],"answer":0,"hint":"Research starts with a question you want to answer.","explain":"Research means finding out facts to answer a question."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What is a source?","choices":["Where facts come from","The answer to a sum","A kind of notes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Books and experts are both one of these.","explain":"A source is where facts come from: a non-fiction book, an expert, or something you saw."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Good notes about 'Snails eat leaves and fruit in the garden' are…","choices":["leaves, fruit","Snails eat leaves and fruit in the garden","and, in, the"],"answer":0,"hint":"Notes are a few key words, not the whole sentence.","explain":"'leaves, fruit' are the key words. Copying the whole sentence is not notes, and small words are not key words."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Dad saw {n} ducks on the pond. We are finding out where ducks live. Can we use what Dad saw?","choices":["Yes, it is his own experience","No, only books count","No, it is an opinion"],"answer":0,"hint":"Things people really saw can be a source too.","explain":"Yes. Dad really saw {n} ducks on a pond, so it is a fact from his own experience.","params":{"n":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which one should NOT go on our class fact poster?","choices":["Rabbits are the best pets!","Rabbits eat grass.","Rabbits live in burrows."],"answer":0,"hint":"One of these is how someone feels.","explain":"'The best pets' is an opinion. A fact poster has only facts."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"We found {n} facts. Which one answers 'How long do cats sleep?'","choices":["Cats sleep for a lot of the day.","Cats have whiskers.","Cats drink water."],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the fact about sleep.","explain":"Only 'Cats sleep for a lot of the day.' is about sleep. The other facts are true but answer other questions.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What do we do first in a shared research project?","choices":["Ask a research question","Make the poster","Write notes"],"answer":0,"hint":"You need to know what you are finding out.","explain":"First we ask a research question. Then we look in sources, make notes and share."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which one is a research question?","choices":["How do bees make honey?","Bees","Are bees nice?"],"answer":0,"hint":"A research question asks something, and facts can answer it.","explain":"'How do bees make honey?' has a fact for an answer. 'Bees' is only a topic, and 'Are bees nice?' asks for an opinion."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Our class found {n} facts about frogs. Our question is 'What do frogs eat?'. Which fact do we keep?","choices":["Frogs eat insects.","Frogs lay eggs.","Frogs can be green."],"answer":0,"hint":"Keep only the facts that answer our question about food.","explain":"Out of the {n} facts, we keep 'Frogs eat insects.' because it is about food. The others are true but answer other questions.","params":{"n":[4,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which is NOT a good source of facts about real lions?","choices":["A story about a lion who talks","A non-fiction book about lions","A zoo keeper"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one is made up, and so not real facts?","explain":"A story is made up, so a talking lion is not a fact. A non-fiction book and a zoo keeper give real facts."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"{n} children all found 'owls hunt at night'. How many times does it go on our poster?","choices":["Once","Once for each child","Not at all"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does saying it again tell us anything new?","explain":"Once. It is one fact, even if {n} children found it. Repeating it adds nothing new.","params":{"n":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Research means finding out. We start with a question, like: what do hedgehogs eat? A good research question has facts for an answer.","Next we look in sources. A source is where facts come from. A fact book is a source. So is an expert, like a park ranger. What you saw yourself counts too. A story book is made up, so it is not a source of facts.","A book has lots of facts. We only keep the ones that answer our question. 'Hedgehogs eat beetles' helps. 'Hedgehogs have spines' is true, but it is not about food.","Then we write notes: just a few key words, like beetles, worms, slugs. Last, we put all our facts together on one class poster, each fact once."],"adult":["This is the early research strand of writing. US Common Core asks children from kindergarten to Grade 2 to participate in shared research and writing projects (W.K.7 to W.2.7) and to recall information from experiences or gather information from provided sources to answer a question (W.K.8 to W.2.8). The English National Curriculum adds retrieving and recording information from non-fiction in Years 3 and 4. The vocabulary is research question, source, fact, non-fiction, notes and key words.","The skill is a cycle: ask a question, choose sources, pick out relevant facts, record them briefly, then share them as a group. Young children's own experience is a legitimate source ('Grandpa saw a hedgehog eat a slug'), and so is asking an adult who knows; what matters is that the information is real and answers the question.","Common mistakes: choosing a topic word ('Hedgehogs') or an opinion question ('Do you like hedgehogs?') instead of a question with a factual answer; treating a story book as a source of facts; collecting every interesting fact, even ones that do not answer the question; copying whole sentences as notes; and repeating the same fact or adding opinions ('the cutest') to the group's writing.","In the lab your child picks a research question, checks four sources with a magnifier, sorts facts that answer the question from true facts that do not, taps key words to make notes, and builds a class poster that leaves off a repeat and an opinion."]},"familyTip":"Pick a question at bedtime, like 'How long do cats sleep?'. Next day, find out together from one non-fiction book or website and one person who knows. Write three key-word notes on a sticky note and share what you found at dinner.","keyIdea":"Research means finding out facts to answer a question. A shared research project starts with a research question that facts can answer, like 'What do hedgehogs eat?'. We gather information from sources: non-fiction books, experts, and things we have seen ourselves, but not made-up stories. We keep only the facts that answer the question, jot them down as notes in a few key words, and put them together as a group, once each, with no opinions.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Our question is 'Where do penguins live?'. Does each fact answer it? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Answers our question","True, but not about where"],"items":[["Penguins live near the South Pole.",0],["Some penguins live on ice.",0],["Penguins live in the sea and on land.",0],["Some penguins live on warm beaches.",0],["Penguins cannot fly.",1],["Penguins eat fish.",1],["Penguin chicks are fluffy.",1],["Penguins are good swimmers.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagresearch","caption":"Flagship lab: choose a research question, check sources with a magnifier, keep facts that answer it, tap key words for notes and build the class poster.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_PJyCGJz5Hv":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Why say your sentence two or three times?","choices":["So I remember it while I write","So it gets longer","So I do not have to write it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what happens while your hand is busy writing.","explain":"Saying it again and again helps it stay in your head while you write."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"How many words are in “We like to swim.”?","choices":["4","13","1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Put up a finger for each word, not each letter.","explain":"We, like, to, swim: 4 words. 13 is the number of letters."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"You said: “My dog is big.” Which did you write the right way?","choices":["My dog is big.","My big is dog.","My cat is big."],"answer":0,"hint":"Same words, same order as you said.","explain":"'My dog is big.' has the same words in the same order. The others mix up or change a word."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"You finish writing. What do you do next?","choices":["Read it back","Put it away","Rub it all out"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check that the page says what you said.","explain":"Read it back to check it says what you meant, and fix any missing word."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Sara says: “I have {n} red pens.” How many words is that?","answer":"5","hint":"Put up a finger for each word. The number counts as one word too.","explain":"I, have, {n}, red, pens: 5 words, so Sara will write 5 words.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Tom said: “The bus is red.” He wrote: “The bus red.” Which word is missing?","choices":["is","bus","red"],"answer":0,"hint":"Read each word he wrote and say each word he said, side by side.","explain":"He said 'The bus is red' but wrote 'The bus red'. The small word 'is' is missing."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Your sentence has {n} words. You have written 2 of them. How many more words to write?","answer":"n - 2","where":"n >= 3","hint":"Count your fingers: how many words are still to go?","explain":"{n} words take away the 2 you wrote leaves {=n-2} more to write.","params":{"n":[3,7]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Before writing a sentence, what should you do?","choices":["Say my sentence out loud","Start writing straight away","Draw a big box"],"answer":0,"hint":"Your words need to be in your head before your pencil moves.","explain":"Say it out loud first. Then you know all the words before you write."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Your sentence has {n} words. You put up one finger for each word. How many fingers?","answer":"n","hint":"One finger for each word.","explain":"One finger per word, so {n} words need {n} fingers.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Zoe said: “I can see the moon.” She wrote: “I see the moon.” What went wrong?","choices":["The word can is missing","Nothing went wrong","She wrote too many words"],"answer":0,"hint":"Read what she wrote and say what she said. Find the gap.","explain":"She said 'I can see', but wrote 'I see'. Reading it back finds the missing 'can'."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Leo counted {n} words when he said his sentence. He wrote {=n-1} words. How many words are missing?","answer":"1","hint":"Compare the words he said with the words he wrote.","explain":"He said {n} words and wrote one fewer, so 1 word is missing. Reading it back finds it.","params":{"n":[3,7]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Before you write, say your sentence out loud. The dog runs to the park. Now say it again.","Say it two or three times. Then it stays in your head while your hand writes.","Say it slowly and put up a finger for each word. The cat naps has three words, so you need three words on the page.","Write the words in the same order you said them. Then read it back. Did a word go missing? Put it in."],"adult":["In England, Year 1 pupils are taught to write sentences by saying out loud what they are going to write about and composing a sentence orally before writing it, then re-reading what they have written to check that it makes sense. Teachers call this oral rehearsal. US Common Core links the same habit to speaking in complete sentences and to drawing, dictating and writing (SL.K.6, W.K.2).","Young writers spend so much effort on letter formation and spelling that the sentence they meant can slip out of working memory mid-way. Saying it two or three times, counting the words on fingers, and saying each word as it is written all reduce that load. Many classes rehearse with a talk partner first, so the sentence is heard and improved before a pencil moves.","Common mistakes: starting to write with no sentence in mind, so it trails off or changes half-way; leaving out small words such as 'and', 'to' or 'the'; writing the words in a different order from the one said; counting letters or syllables instead of words; and never re-reading. A missing word is the slip that re-reading catches most often.","In the lab your child says a sentence three times, taps a finger for each word of three sentences, writes two sentences word by word in spoken order, finds the missing word in three written sentences, and writes two sentences from memory after hiding them."]},"familyTip":"Before your child writes a card or a list, ask them to tell you the sentence first. Say it back together twice, count the words on fingers, then write. When they finish, ask them to read it to you with a finger under each word.","keyIdea":"Before you write a sentence, say it out loud. Say it two or three times so it sticks in your head. Count the words on your fingers so you know how many to write. Write the words in the order you said them, then read it back to check it says what you said.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Say it first, or not? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🗣️ Good before writing","✗ Not a good start"],"items":[["Say my sentence out loud",0],["Say it two or three times",0],["Count the words on my fingers",0],["Tell a partner my sentence",0],["Start writing with no idea",1],["Count the letters",1],["Skip reading it back",1],["Change the words while I write",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagoral","caption":"Flagship lab: say a sentence 3 times, count its words on fingers, write it in spoken order, find the missing word, and write from memory.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_nDAcXoPa0c":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which part of a story tells who it is about and where?","choices":["The beginning","The ending","No part"],"answer":0,"hint":"Readers need to know this before anything happens.","explain":"The beginning tells who and where, so the reader knows what is going on."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"“Then the dog ran away. First, I got a dog.” What is wrong?","choices":["The order is mixed up","Nothing is wrong","It needs more dogs"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can the dog run away before you get it?","explain":"You get the dog first, then it runs away. The sentences are in the wrong order."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"The middle is “I lost my hat.” Which ending fits?","choices":["Finally, I found it under my bed.","Finally, I ate my lunch.","First, I had a hat."],"answer":0,"hint":"The ending should finish the problem from the middle.","explain":"Finding the hat solves the problem of losing it. Lunch has nothing to do with the hat."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Ben writes a story of {n} sentences. The first sentence is the beginning and the last is the ending. How many are in the middle?","answer":"n-2","hint":"Take away the beginning and the ending.","explain":"{n} sentences take away 1 beginning and 1 ending leaves {=n-2} in the middle.","params":{"n":[4,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Can a story end with “I felt happy”?","choices":["Yes, a feeling can end it","No, endings cannot have feelings","Only at the beginning"],"answer":0,"hint":"How someone felt is one way to finish a story.","explain":"Telling how someone felt at the end is a good ending. It shows the story is finished."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which word tells the reader the story is ending?","choices":["Finally","First","Once"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word means last of all?","explain":"Finally means last of all, so it shows the story is ending."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"“First, we baked a cake. Then we put it in the oven.” What is missing?","choices":["An ending","A beginning","A time word"],"answer":0,"hint":"We know how it starts. Do we know how it finishes?","explain":"It has a beginning and a middle, but no ending. We need to know what happened to the cake."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A story has a beginning, {n} middle sentences and an ending. How many sentences come before the ending?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Count the beginning and the middle sentences. The ending comes last.","explain":"The beginning and the {n} middle sentences come first: 1 + {n} = {=n+1} sentences before the ending.","params":{"n":[1,4]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A simple story has a beginning, a middle and an ending. Which sentence is the ending?","choices":["Finally, we got home and I felt happy.","First, we went to the zoo.","Then we saw a lion."],"answer":0,"hint":"The ending tells how the story finishes. Look for Finally.","explain":"Finally, we got home and I felt happy finishes the story and tells how I felt, so it is the ending."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A story has a beginning, {n} middle sentences and an ending. How many sentences does it have?","answer":"n+2","hint":"Add the beginning and the ending to the middle sentences.","explain":"1 beginning + {n} middle + 1 ending makes {=n+2} sentences.","params":{"n":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which time word is best for the first sentence of a story?","choices":["First","Finally","Then"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word means at the very start?","explain":"First tells the reader this is the start. Then and Finally come later."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Your story has {n} sentences. The last one is the ending. Which sentence number is the ending?","answer":"n","hint":"The ending is the very last sentence.","explain":"With {n} sentences, the last one is sentence {n}, and that is the ending.","params":{"n":[3,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A story has three parts. The beginning tells who and where. The middle tells what happened. The ending tells how it finished.","Tell the parts in order. First I planted a seed. Then I watered it. Finally a sunflower grew. If you mix them up, it sounds silly.","Time words help. Use First at the start, Then in the middle, and Finally at the end.","The ending must fit the middle. If the dog knocked over your ice cream, the ending says what happened next, or how you felt."],"adult":["This is early narrative writing. In Year 1 the National Curriculum asks pupils to sequence sentences to form short narratives, and in Year 2 to write narratives about personal experiences and those of others. US Common Core W.1.3 asks for two or more appropriately sequenced events, temporal words to signal event order, and some sense of closure; W.K.3 adds a reaction to what happened.","Teachers call the order of events sequencing and the order words temporal words or time connectives (first, next, then, after that, finally). A story map with three boxes, beginning, middle and end, helps children plan before they write. The ending gives closure: it solves the problem from the middle or tells how the character felt.","Common mistakes: listing events with 'and then' over and over with no ending, putting events in the wrong order, using Finally or First in the wrong place, stopping before the problem is solved, and adding an ending that starts something new or has nothing to do with the middle. Ask 'how does it end?' and 'does your ending finish the problem?'","In the lab your child puts picture-sentence cards in order, fills gaps with First, Then and Finally, swaps mixed-up sentences back, picks the ending that finishes a story, and builds two stories whose endings match their middles."]},"familyTip":"At bedtime tell the story of your day in three parts. Say 'First…', then let your child say 'Then…', and ask them for the 'Finally…' with how someone felt. Swap roles the next night.","keyIdea":"A simple story has a beginning, a middle and an ending, told in the order they happened. The beginning says who and what is going on. The middle says what happened, often a problem. The ending finishes it, with what happened in the end or how someone felt. Time words like first, then and finally show the order. If you mix the sentences up, the story stops making sense.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Beginning or ending? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🚪 Beginning","🏁 Ending"],"items":[["First, we went to the zoo.",0],["One day, a fox lived in a wood.",0],["First, I got my new bike.",0],["Once, Ali went to the beach.",0],["Finally, we went home happy.",1],["In the end, the fox was safe.",1],["Finally, I could ride with no help.",1],["At last, Ali found the shell.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtale","caption":"Flagship lab: put story cards in order, choose First, Then and Finally, fix mixed-up stories, pick endings and build your own.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_vauULTecMH":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which link fits best between \"They got on the bus\" and \"The race began\"?","choices":["Twenty minutes later,","Suddenly,","A year ago,"],"answer":0,"hint":"How much time passed, and was it a surprise?","explain":"Twenty minutes later shows a short, normal jump. Suddenly is for surprises, and a year ago jumps backwards."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which sentence shows a character is nervous instead of telling it?","choices":["His hands shook as he held the card.","He was nervous.","He felt very nervous indeed."],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one could you picture or film?","explain":"Shaking hands show the feeling. The other two just name it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Leo's problem is that he lost his dog. Which ending is best?","choices":["Leo spots Biscuit by the pond and hugs him tight.","Leo wakes up. It was all a dream.","Then a storm starts and the park floods."],"answer":0,"hint":"Which ending answers the problem?","explain":"Finding Biscuit answers the problem. A dream cancels the story, and a storm is a new problem."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"You plan {n} paragraphs. One is the opening and one is the ending. How many paragraphs are in the middle?","answer":"n-2","hint":"Take away the opening and the ending.","explain":"{n} paragraphs minus the opening and the ending leaves {=n-2} middle paragraphs.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"What makes a slow-motion moment work?","choices":["A mix of action, speech, thoughts and senses","Five actions in a row","Lots of \"and then\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the four tools: action, speech, thought and senses.","explain":"Mixing the tools puts the reader inside the moment. Repeating one kind, or chaining \"and then\", reads like a list."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Why write the car ride in one line?","choices":["Nothing changes there, so the reader reaches the problem sooner","Summaries are always lazy","Readers want every detail"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does anything change during the car ride?","explain":"A summary is a choice. It skips time where nothing changes, so the scenes stand out."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A story covers {n} days. You add one time phrase at each jump from one day to the next. How many time phrases do you need?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Count the gaps between the days, not the days.","explain":"{n} days have {=n-1} gaps between them, so you need {=n-1} time phrases.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"When is \"Suddenly\" the right word?","choices":["When something unexpected happens","At the start of every paragraph","Whenever time moves on"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does suddenly mean? Is the event a surprise?","explain":"Suddenly signals a surprise. Using it for ordinary jumps wears it out."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"What does good story dialogue do?","choices":["Shows a feeling or moves the action","Copies everything people really say","Fills space between events"],"answer":0,"hint":"Why include a line at all?","explain":"Each line should reveal character or push the plot on. Real small talk is cut."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which part of a story about a lost kite deserves a slow scene?","choices":["The moment the string slips out of Ava's hand","Ava eating breakfast","The walk to the hill"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where does the problem start?","explain":"The string slipping is the problem, so slow it down. Breakfast and the walk change nothing, so summarise them."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Your story has {n} events. You make 2 of them slow scenes. How many events are quick summaries?","answer":"n-2","hint":"Every event is either a scene or a summary.","explain":"{n} events take away 2 scenes leaves {=n-2} summaries.","params":{"n":[4,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which line of dialogue does a job in the story?","choices":["\"Don't touch the wire!\" Dad shouted.","\"Hi.\" \"Hi.\"","\"It's a nice day.\" \"Yes.\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the line show a feeling or move the action?","explain":"Dad's warning shows fear and changes what happens next. Greetings and weather chat do no job, so cut them."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A scene has {n} lines of dialogue. {k} of them are hellos and weather chat. How many lines are left after you cut that small talk?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Cut only the lines that do no job.","explain":"{n} lines minus {k} lines of small talk leaves {=n-k} lines that do a job.","params":{"n":[5,8],"k":[1,3]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Every story needs a problem. Zara is scared to dive. Tom's dog runs off. The problem is why the reader keeps reading.","Writers choose where to slow down. The moment Zara freezes on the diving block gets a whole scene. The bus ride to the pool gets one line. That is called pacing.","To slow a moment down, mix your tools. Show an action: her toes curl over the edge. Add speech: \"I can't,\" she whispers. Add a thought and a sound or a smell. Do not just write \"she was scared\".","Dialogue in stories is not like real chat. Cut the hellos and weather talk. Keep lines that show a feeling or push the action on. Link the jumps with time phrases such as \"Twenty minutes later\" or \"That evening\". Save \"Suddenly\" for real surprises. At the end, answer the problem. Do not finish with \"it was all a dream\"."],"adult":["This is narrative composition for ages 7 to 11. Common Core W.3.3 asks pupils to establish a situation, use dialogue and descriptions of actions, thoughts and feelings, use temporal words and phrases to signal event order, and provide a sense of closure. W.4.3 adds concrete words and sensory details, and W.5.3 adds pacing. The National Curriculum in England asks Years 5 and 6 to integrate dialogue to convey character and advance the action.","Pacing is the balance of scene and summary. A scene is told moment by moment, close to real time, and belongs at the problem and the turning point. A summary covers time quickly. Adverbials of time such as 'twenty minutes later' or 'that evening' make the jumps clear and build cohesion across paragraphs.","Common mistakes: equal detail for every event, so the story reads like a list joined by 'and then'; dialogue that copies real small talk; naming a feeling ('she was scared') instead of showing it; 'suddenly' used for events that are not surprising; and endings that cancel the problem (a dream) or open a new one.","In the lab your child chooses which events become scenes, builds the key moment from mixed action, speech, thought and sense lines, keeps only dialogue that does a job, links the gaps with fitting time phrases, and picks endings that answer the problem."]},"familyTip":"After reading a chapter together, ask: which moment did the author slow right down, and which part did they skip in one line? Then ask your child to retell a day out, spending most words on the one moment that went wrong.","keyIdea":"A good story has a problem and spends its words where things change. Slow down the key moment into a scene, mixing action, dialogue, thoughts and sensory details. Summarise the parts where nothing changes in a line, and link events with time phrases that show how far the story jumped. Keep only dialogue that shows a feeling or moves the action, and end by answering the problem.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Scene or summary? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Slow scene","Quick summary"],"items":[["The rope snaps as Kai reaches the top",0],["Mia opens the letter with shaking hands",0],["The moment the lights go out in the museum",0],["The goalkeeper dives for the last penalty",0],["They drove to Gran's house",1],["Next morning they packed the car",1],["The week passed quietly",1],["After lunch they walked home",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagpace","caption":"Flagship lab: pace scenes and summaries, build a slow moment, keep dialogue that works, link gaps and end on the problem.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt__MDiDU9Vck":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"How should an information page end?","choices":["Now you know how bees live.","The end.","Once upon a time."],"answer":0,"hint":"Stories end with \"The end.\" What does information do?","explain":"A closing sentence sums up the topic. \"The end\" is for stories."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Where does the heading go?","choices":["At the top","At the bottom","After the facts"],"answer":0,"hint":"The heading tells you the topic first.","explain":"The heading goes at the top, so the reader knows the topic before the facts."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which line does not belong on a page about fish?","choices":["My brother has a red coat.","Fish live in water.","Fish use fins to swim."],"answer":0,"hint":"Which line is not about fish?","explain":"The coat sentence is about something else. The other two are fish facts."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"\"I think owls are pretty.\" What is it?","choices":["A feeling","A fact","A label"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does it start with \"I think\"?","explain":"\"I think\" tells a feeling. A fact is true for everyone."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"You draw a butterfly and add {n} labels. Then you add 1 more label for the legs. How many labels now?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Count on one more from {n}.","explain":"{n} labels and 1 more make {=n+1} labels.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A label says \"wing\". Where should it point?","choices":["To the wing","To the leg","To the sky"],"answer":0,"hint":"Labels point to the part they name.","explain":"A label points to its own part, so \"wing\" points to the wing."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Mia writes {n} sentences about trains. {k} of them are about her lunch. How many are about trains?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take away the lunch lines.","explain":"{n} take away {k} lunch sentences leaves {=n-k} sentences about trains.","params":{"n":[5,6],"k":[1,2]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which is information writing?","choices":["How to make a jam sandwich","A story about a dragon","A poem about the moon"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one tells true facts or steps?","explain":"Steps for a jam sandwich tell true things. The dragon story and the poem do not inform."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which is the best first sentence for a page about bees?","choices":["Bees are insects that make honey.","Once upon a time there was a bee.","I like ice cream."],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one tells the reader the topic?","explain":"\"Bees are insects that make honey\" names the topic and gives a fact. The others are a story and something else."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Sam writes {n} facts about cats and one closing sentence. How many sentences is that?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Add the closing sentence to the facts.","explain":"{n} facts and 1 closing sentence make {=n+1} sentences.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which sentence is a fact?","choices":["Dogs have four legs.","Dogs are the best pets.","My dog can drive a car."],"answer":0,"hint":"A fact is true for every dog.","explain":"Dogs have four legs is true. \"The best\" is a feeling, and a dog driving is made up."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A page about frogs has {n} sentences. One says \"I had cake.\" How many sentences are about frogs?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Take away the sentence that is not about frogs.","explain":"{n} take away the cake sentence leaves {=n-1} sentences about frogs. Cut the cake one!","params":{"n":[3,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Some writing tells a story. Some writing tells true facts. When you write to inform, you tell a reader facts about one topic, like frogs or bees.","Start by naming the topic. Put a heading at the top, like \"Frogs\". Then write a first line: \"Frogs live in and near ponds.\" Now the reader knows what your page is about.","Next, write facts. A fact is true. \"Tadpoles grow into frogs\" is a fact. \"I think frogs are cute\" is a feeling, so leave it out. Every line must be about frogs.","You can draw a picture and add labels, like \"wing\" or \"leg\". Each label points to its part. At the end, write a closing line, like \"Now you know how frogs grow.\" Do not write \"The end.\" That is for stories."],"adult":["In the English National Curriculum, Year 1 and Year 2 pupils write for different purposes, including non-fiction, and sequence sentences to form short texts. US Common Core W.K.2 and W.1.2 ask children to write informative or explanatory texts. They name a topic, supply some facts and provide some sense of closure. Drawing and dictating count at the start.","An information text has a heading, an opening sentence that names the topic, a few facts, and often a labelled picture. It ends with a closing sentence. Children also learn what to leave out: opinions ('the best', 'I think'), made-up details and sentences about something else.","Common mistakes: drifting into a story ('Once upon a time a frog…'), ending with 'The end.', mixing in feelings as if they were facts, and adding a sentence about the writer's day. Labels on the wrong part are a slip too. Be careful not to mark a child down for spelling at this stage. The aim is facts in a clear order, said aloud or written.","In the lab your child picks first sentences that name a topic, sorts frog sentences into facts and not-facts, and taps the off-topic sentence on two pages. They label a butterfly's wing, antenna, body and leg. Then they build a frog page in order: heading, topic sentence, two facts and a closing sentence."]},"familyTip":"Pick something your child knows well, like how to brush teeth or what the dog eats. Ask them to tell you three facts while you write them down, then read it back and ask: is every sentence about the topic? How shall we finish?","keyIdea":"Writing to inform means telling a reader true facts about one topic. An information text names the topic first, often with a heading and an opening line like \"Frogs live in and near ponds.\" Then it gives facts, keeps every line on the topic and leaves out feelings and made-up bits. Labels on a picture are facts too. A closing sentence sums up, instead of \"The end.\"","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Information or story? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["📘 Information (facts)","📖 Story"],"items":[["Bees make honey.",0],["Owls hunt at night.",0],["Plants need water.",0],["A cat has four legs.",0],["Once upon a time a bee met a king.",1],["The dragon flew over the hill.",1],["Then the frog said, \"Let's go!\"",1],["They lived happily ever after.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaginform","caption":"Flagship lab: name the topic, sort facts from feelings, cut the off-topic sentence, label a butterfly and build an information page in order.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_FGCFUCqJBB":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A mouse is sneaking past a sleeping cat. Which verb fits best?","choices":["tiptoed","thundered","went"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say each one aloud. Which shows the mouse is being careful?","explain":"Tiptoed shows the mouse moving quietly. Thundered is loud, and went does not show sneaking at all."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which conjunction fits? \"We stayed inside ___ it was raining.\"","choices":["because","so","but"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the rain the reason or the result?","explain":"The rain is the reason, so because fits. So would make the rain a result of staying in, and but suggests a surprise."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which version sounds best in a story?","choices":["The owl swooped down onto the field.","The big dark grey soft quiet owl went down very very fast.","The owl went. It went down. It went to the field."],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a strong verb and no pile of extra words.","explain":"Swooped is a strong verb that does the work. The second version piles up adjectives, and the third repeats went three times."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Every line in Dan's story starts with \"Then I\". What is the best fix?","choices":["Say some sentences aloud in new ways, such as with dialogue or a joining word","Write the same story more quickly","Add more sentences that start \"Then I\""],"answer":0,"hint":"The problem is that every line has the same shape.","explain":"Rehearsing new versions aloud gives a mix of sentence shapes. Writing faster or adding more of the same does not fix it."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A story has {n} sentences. {k} of them are dialogue. How many sentences are not dialogue?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take the dialogue sentences away from the total.","explain":"{n} sentences take away {k} lines of dialogue leaves {=n-k} sentences that are not dialogue.","params":{"n":[6,10],"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which words go inside the inverted commas? Mum said Come and eat","choices":["Come and eat","Mum said","Mum said Come and eat"],"answer":0,"hint":"Inverted commas hold only the words that are spoken.","explain":"Mum spoke the words \"Come and eat\", so they go inside: Mum said, \"Come and eat.\" The words Mum said stay outside."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Asha tries {n} different openings for her idea aloud, then {k} more. How many versions has she tried?","answer":"n+k","hint":"Add the first tries and the extra tries.","explain":"{n} tries and {k} more make {=n+k} versions to choose from.","params":{"n":[2,4],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which sentence joins two clauses with a conjunction?","choices":["The bell rang, so we lined up.","The bell rang.","\"Line up!\" said Miss Khan."],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a joining word such as so, because, but or when.","explain":"So joins \"The bell rang\" and \"we lined up\" into one sentence. The others are a short sentence and a line of dialogue."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Why should you rehearse a sentence aloud before you write it, and try varying it?","choices":["To hear if it sounds right and pick the best version","To make every sentence as long as possible","So you can skip the full stop"],"answer":0,"hint":"What can you notice when you hear a sentence out loud?","explain":"Saying it aloud lets you hear it, try another way and keep the version that works best. Long is not the aim, and full stops are still needed."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Kai says his sentence aloud {n} times, with a different verb each time. He writes down only the best one. How many versions does he not use?","answer":"n-1","hint":"He keeps one version. Take that one away from the total.","explain":"He tried {n} versions and kept 1, so {=n-1} versions were not used. Trying them still helped him choose.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which line of dialogue is punctuated correctly?","choices":["\"Is it time yet?\" asked Ravi.","\"Is it time yet? asked Ravi.\"","Is it time yet? \"asked Ravi.\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Which words did Ravi actually say?","explain":"Ravi said \"Is it time yet?\", so only those words and the question mark go inside the inverted commas. \"Asked Ravi\" stays outside."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A paragraph has {n} short sentences. Lily joins {k} pairs of them with because or so. How many sentences does the paragraph have now?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Each join turns two sentences into one, so each join takes away one sentence.","explain":"Each of the {k} joins turns two sentences into one, so {n} − {k} = {=n-k} sentences are left.","params":{"n":[6,9],"k":[1,3]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Say your words out loud first, then put it on paper. This is called rehearsing. When you hear it, you can tell if it sounds right. Then you can try it another way and pick the best one.","Try different words. The fox went across the garden. Is that the best word? Say crept, then stomped. Crept shows the fox is sneaking. Stomped would wake everyone up. Keep the word that fits what is happening.","Try different shapes too. Two short ideas can join with a conjunction: Mia put on her coat because she was cold. Because gives a reason. So gives a result. But shows a problem. When gives a time.","When someone talks, say their words in their voice first. Then write them inside inverted commas: \"Let's go!\" shouted Tom. Only the spoken words go inside, with the ! or ? too. A good story mixes short lines, joined lines and talk."],"adult":["The English National Curriculum asks Year 3 and 4 pupils to draft by composing and rehearsing sentences orally, including dialogue, and to build a varied and rich vocabulary and an increasing range of sentence structures. The grammar for these years adds conjunctions such as when, if, because and although, and inverted commas for direct speech. US Common Core L.3.1 and L.3.3 cover simple, compound and complex sentences and choosing words for effect.","Oral rehearsal works because a child can hold, test and change a line ahead of the slow work of handwriting and spelling. Ask for two or three versions of the same idea, said aloud, and talk about which one works best and why. The reason matters more than the choice.","Common mistakes: putting inverted commas round the whole line, including the reporting clause ('called Dad'); choosing an impressive verb that does not fit, such as 'thundered' for a sneaking mouse; using 'so' where the second clause is the reason; and piling up adjectives in the belief that longer is better. Watch too for a page where every line has the same shape.","In the lab your child marks the inverted commas in three lines of dialogue and tries verbs aloud for three moments. They join four pairs of ideas with because, so, but or when, and pick the clearest of three versions twice. Last, they rewrite a short story so it mixes a short line, a joined line and dialogue."]},"familyTip":"At dinner, pick one thing that happened today and take turns saying it three different ways: a short version, a version joined with because or when, and a version with someone speaking. Vote on which one you would write down, and say why.","keyIdea":"Rehearsing means saying your words aloud first, then putting it on paper. Saying it lets you try two or three versions and keep the best: a verb that fits the moment (crept, whispered, yelled), a conjunction that keeps the meaning (because, so, but, when) and inverted commas round only the words someone says. Clear beats long, and a mix of short sentences, joined sentences and dialogue keeps a reader interested.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Short, joined or dialogue? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["✂️ Short sentence","🔗 Joined with a conjunction","💬 Dialogue"],"items":[["The door creaked.",0],["Ali ran home.",0],["We left early because it was snowing.",1],["The bell rang, so we lined up.",1],["I was hungry, but tea was not ready.",1],["\"Wait for me!\" called Priya.",2],["Gran asked, \"Who wants cake?\"",2],["\"Shh,\" whispered Leo.",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagrehearse","caption":"Flagship lab: mark dialogue, try verbs aloud, join ideas with conjunctions, pick the clearest version and mix sentence shapes.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_FW9_8F52bw":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which is a digital source?","choices":["A science agency website","A printed encyclopedia","A paper atlas"],"answer":0,"hint":"Digital sources are found on a screen.","explain":"A website is a digital source. An encyclopedia and an atlas on paper are print sources."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why leave out a forum post with no name and no date?","choices":["You cannot check who wrote it or if it is up to date","It is too short","Websites are never true"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do you need to know about a source to trust it?","explain":"Without a name or a date you cannot check the writer or whether the facts are current. Many websites are trustworthy when you can check who made them."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What is wrong with copying a whole sentence into your notes?","choices":["It is not in your own words, so it is not a note","Nothing, it saves time","Sentences are always wrong"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does a note look like?","explain":"A note is key words in your own words. Copying is not your work, and it skips the thinking that helps you understand."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Two sources give different facts about the same thing. What do you do?","choices":["Check a third trusted source","Use the one you found first","Write down whichever sounds best"],"answer":0,"hint":"How can you find out which one is right?","explain":"When sources disagree, a third trusted source helps you see which fact holds up."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"What should go in your list of sources?","choices":["The title and who made each source","Just 'the internet'","Only the books, not websites"],"answer":0,"hint":"Someone else should be able to find the same source.","explain":"Give the title and who made each source, books and websites alike, so a reader can find and check it."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"You checked {n} facts in two sources. {k} of them needed a third source because the first two disagreed. How many facts did the two sources agree on?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take away the facts that needed more checking.","explain":"{n} - {k} = {=n-k} facts where both sources agreed.","params":{"n":[4,8],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Your list of sources has {p} print sources and {d} digital sources. How many sources are on the list?","answer":"p+d","hint":"Add the print and the digital sources.","explain":"{p} print + {d} digital = {=p+d} sources on the list.","params":{"p":[1,4],"d":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"You have the note 'ground swells up before eruptions'. Which question does it answer?","choices":["What warns people that a volcano may erupt?","Where are most volcanoes found?","Which volcano is the biggest?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the note about a place, a size or a sign?","explain":"Swelling ground is a warning sign, so the note goes under the warning question."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Your topic is rainforests. Which is the best research question?","choices":["Why do rainforests get so much rain?","Are rainforests nice?","Tell me everything about rainforests."],"answer":0,"hint":"Pick a question that facts can answer and that is small enough for a short project.","explain":"'Why do rainforests get so much rain?' can be answered with facts. 'Nice' asks for an opinion, and 'everything' is the whole topic."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"You plan {q} research questions and want 2 sources for each one. How many sources is that, if none are shared?","answer":"2*q","hint":"Each question needs 2 sources. Multiply.","explain":"{q} questions with 2 sources each make {=2*q} sources.","params":{"q":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"The source says: 'Most bats hunt at night.' Which note keeps the fact right?","choices":["most bats: hunt at night","bats only come out at night","Most bats hunt at night."],"answer":0,"hint":"Use key words, and do not change 'most' into something else.","explain":"'most bats: hunt at night' is short and keeps 'most'. 'Only' changes the fact, and the last one is copied word for word."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You have {a} notes under 'Where?' and {b} notes under 'Why?'. How many notes is that in all?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the notes in both categories.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} notes, sorted into 2 categories.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A research project starts with a topic, like volcanoes. A topic is too big to answer in one go. So split it into questions. Where are volcanoes found? Why do they erupt? How do people know one is coming? Each question looks at a different part.","Next, find sources. Print sources are on paper, like an encyclopedia or an atlas. Digital sources are online, like a science website or a video by an expert. Use sources where you can see who made them and when. A post with no name, a made-up story or an advert is not one to trust for facts.","Take notes as you read. A note is a few key words in your own words, not the whole sentence copied out. Check that your note still says the same fact. If the source says most volcanoes, do not write all volcanoes.","Sort your notes under your questions, so each part of the topic has its own facts. Check big facts in two sources. If they disagree, look at a third trusted source. At the end, list your sources: the title and who made each one."],"adult":["This matches the Common Core writing standards for grades 4 and 5 (W.4.7, W.4.8, W.5.7, W.5.8). Pupils conduct short research projects that build knowledge through investigation of different aspects of a topic. They gather relevant information from print and digital sources, take notes and categorize information, and provide a list of sources. In grade 5 they also summarize or paraphrase information in notes and finished work. In England the same skills sit in the Key Stage 2 programme: retrieve, record and present information from non-fiction, and note and develop ideas, drawing on reading and research.","The useful terms are research question, aspect, print source, digital source, reliable source, key words, notes, paraphrase (the same meaning in your own words), summarize (only the main points), categorize, cross-check and list of sources. A good research question is open and answerable from evidence. A feeling question, a yes/no question or a whole topic is not.","Common mistakes: asking about the whole topic, or asking opinion questions. Trusting the first page found, or thinking any book beats any website. Copying whole sentences, which is plagiarism and skips the thinking. Changing the meaning while shortening a note, so 'most' becomes 'all'. And writing 'the internet' or 'Google' as a source.","In the lab your child plans three questions about volcanoes and sees why an opinion, a yes/no question, 'everything' or a repeat is refused. They sort seven print and digital sources and pick the best of three notes, where the copy and the changed fact are both rejected. Then they file notes under headings and cross-check three facts in two sources."]},"familyTip":"Pick a topic your child is curious about and ask them for three questions about different parts of it. When they find a fact online, ask: who made this page, and where else can we check it?","keyIdea":"A short research project builds knowledge about one topic by looking at different parts of it. Plan a few research questions that facts can answer, each about a different aspect. Gather information from print and digital sources you can check: you can see who made them and when. Take brief notes in key words and your own words, without changing the facts. Sort the notes into categories that match your questions. Check important facts in a second source, and look further when sources disagree. Finish with a list of sources, giving the title and who made each one.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Good research habit or not? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["✅ Good research habit","⚠️ Fix this habit"],"items":[["Ask: Why do volcanoes erupt?",0],["Ask: Are volcanoes cool?",1],["Note key words in your own words",0],["Copy the whole paragraph",1],["Check a big fact in two sources",0],["Use the first page you find",1],["List each source's title and author",0],["Write 'the internet' as your source",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagproj","caption":"Flagship lab: plan three volcano questions, sort print and digital sources, make notes in your own words, file them by question and cross-check facts.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_AvJMWQbDsr":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which is the best sub-heading for a paragraph about where owls live?","choices":["Homes","Owls live in barns and trees.","Owls"],"answer":0,"hint":"A sub-heading is a short label for one part, not a sentence.","explain":"Homes is a short label for that one paragraph. A full sentence is not a heading, and Owls names the whole report, so it is the title."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which sentence should open a paragraph about how frogs breathe?","choices":["Frogs can breathe in two ways.","They take in air through their skin.","Frogs lay eggs in ponds."],"answer":0,"hint":"Pick the sentence that tells what the whole paragraph is about.","explain":"\"Frogs can breathe in two ways\" is the topic sentence. The skin fact is a detail, and frog eggs belong in another paragraph."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A paragraph about what cats eat has one sentence about where cats sleep. What should you do?","choices":["Move it to a paragraph about sleep","Leave it there","Make it the title"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each paragraph keeps to one idea.","explain":"The sleep sentence is about a different idea, so it belongs in its own paragraph about where cats sleep."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"\"Meanwhile, back at the castle, the queen was waiting.\" Which TiPToP reasons start this paragraph?","choices":["Time and place","A new person speaking","None, it carries on"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for when and where words.","explain":"\"Meanwhile\" signals a change of time and \"back at the castle\" a change of place, so it needs a new paragraph."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A report on sharks has the title Sharks and {n} paragraphs, each with its own sub-heading. How many headings are there in all, counting the title?","answer":"n+1","hint":"One title, plus one sub-heading for each paragraph.","explain":"{n} sub-headings plus 1 title make {=n+1} headings.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Amy says, \"Can I come?\" Then Raj answers, \"Yes, hurry!\" How should Raj's words be set out?","choices":["On a new line, in a new paragraph","In the same paragraph as Amy","Under a sub-heading"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happens each time a new person speaks?","explain":"Raj is a new speaker, so his words start a new paragraph. Stories do not usually use sub-headings."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which reason is NOT a good reason to start a new paragraph?","choices":["The last paragraph had four sentences","The story moves to a new place","Something new happens"],"answer":0,"hint":"Paragraphs have no set length.","explain":"Length is not a reason on its own. A new place, time, event or speaker is."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Your report on bees has {a} sentences about their bodies, {b} about making honey and {c} about the hive. If each idea gets one paragraph, how many paragraphs are there?","answer":"3","hint":"Count the ideas: bodies, honey, hive.","explain":"Three ideas make 3 paragraphs, even though there are {=a+b+c} sentences in all.","params":{"a":[2,3],"b":[2,4],"c":[1,3]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"When should you start a new paragraph in a report?","choices":["When the idea changes","After every three sentences","Only at the very end"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what a paragraph is about: one idea.","explain":"A paragraph keeps to one idea, so a new paragraph starts when the idea changes, however long the last one was."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A report has {a} sentences about a dog's looks, then {b} sentences about its food. How many paragraphs should it have?","answer":"2","hint":"Count the ideas, not the sentences.","explain":"There are two ideas, looks and food, so the {=a+b} sentences make 2 paragraphs.","params":{"a":[2,4],"b":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"In a story: \"The next day, Leo went back to the cave.\" Why does this start a new paragraph?","choices":["A new time","A new person speaks","The sentence is long"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first three words.","explain":"\"The next day\" moves the story to a new time, the T in TiPToP."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A story has {n} lines of speech, and each line is said by a different person from the line before. How many new paragraphs do those lines start?","answer":"n","hint":"Each new speaker gets a new paragraph.","explain":"Every change of speaker starts a new paragraph, so {n} lines of speech start {n} paragraphs.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A paragraph is a group of sentences about one idea. In a report on hedgehogs, one paragraph can tell what they look like, the next what they eat, and the next where they live. Each sentence goes with the others about the same idea.","Start a new paragraph when the idea changes. There is no set number of lines. If you write two sentences about a fox's fur and four about its food, that is two paragraphs: one short and one long. On the page, a new paragraph starts on a new line, often set in a little or after a gap.","Stories use paragraphs too. Remember TiPToP: start a new one for a new Time, a new Place, a new Topic, which is something new that happens, or a new Person speaking. Each time someone new speaks, they get a new line.","Information texts can also use headings. The title names the whole topic, like Bats. A sub-heading is a short label for one part, like Food. It is not a full sentence. Open each paragraph with a topic sentence that tells the reader what it is about, then add the details."],"adult":["This is the Key Stage 2 writing work on paragraphs in the national curriculum for England. In Years 3 and 4 pupils organise paragraphs around a theme, and the grammar appendix introduces paragraphs as a way to group related material. In non-narrative writing they use simple organisational devices such as headings and sub-headings. In Years 5 and 6 they build cohesion within and across paragraphs and use further devices to structure text.","Useful terms: paragraph, theme, related material, heading or title, sub-heading, topic sentence and indent. TiPToP (time, place, topic, person) is a common classroom memory aid for when a story needs a new paragraph. The rule for speech is the firmest: each new speaker gets a new paragraph.","Common mistakes: breaking after a set number of lines, or writing one long block. Mixing two themes in one paragraph, such as a hedgehog's food among facts about its home. Using a full sentence or the whole topic name as a sub-heading. Keeping a new speaker's words in the last speaker's paragraph. Opening with a detail instead of saying what the paragraph is about.","In the lab your child sorts six hedgehog sentences into three themed paragraphs, then marks the breaks in a fox report that has no paragraphs and finds they fall after two sentences and after six. They give the TiPToP reason for four breaks in a river story, place a title and three sub-headings over a bat page (one card is a sentence, not a heading) and choose the topic sentence that opens a paragraph."]},"familyTip":"When your child writes a few lines about a day out or a pet, ask them to say the one idea each paragraph is about. If a sentence is about something else, does it need a paragraph of its own?","keyIdea":"A paragraph is a group of sentences about one idea. Start a new paragraph when the idea changes, not after a set number of sentences, so a paragraph can be two sentences or five. In a story, start a new paragraph for a new time, place, topic or person speaking (TiPToP). In non-fiction, a title names the whole topic, a sub-heading names one paragraph, and a topic sentence at the start tells the reader what the paragraph is about.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which paragraph does it belong in? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🦔 What they look like","🐛 What they eat","🍂 Where they live"],"items":[["Brown spines cover their back",0],["A pointed nose and small eyes",0],["Short legs and a tiny tail",0],["Beetles and worms",1],["Slugs from the garden",1],["Hunting insects at night",1],["A nest of dry leaves",2],["Under a hedge or a shed",2],["A log pile in winter",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagpara","caption":"Flagship lab: group hedgehog facts, mark the breaks in a fox report, give TiPToP reasons, place sub-headings and pick topic sentences.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_nYU6x2E2T8":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which key do you hold to type a capital letter?","choices":["Shift","Space bar","Backspace"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is the key with an arrow pointing up.","explain":"Hold Shift and press a letter to make it a capital."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"When should you publish your writing?","choices":["After you have edited it","Before you write a draft","Straight after you make a plan"],"answer":0,"hint":"Publishing is the best copy that readers see.","explain":"You publish after you plan, draft and edit, so readers get your best copy."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"You read “Look out, a shark!” to the class. How should it sound?","choices":["With a strong voice","In a sleepy voice","Like a question"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the mark at the end.","explain":"The exclamation mark shows a strong feeling, so say it with a strong voice."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"You trip on a word while reading aloud. What is best?","choices":["Go back and read it again","Stop and sit down","Skip the rest of the story"],"answer":0,"hint":"Your listeners want to know what the sentence says.","explain":"Going back and reading it again fixes the slip, and the story still makes sense."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"You typed a word with {n} extra letters on the end, and the cursor is just after them. How many times do you press Backspace?","answer":"n","hint":"Each press of Backspace deletes one letter just before the cursor.","explain":"Each press deletes one extra letter, so you press Backspace {n} times.","params":{"n":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Why should you hold your page low when you read aloud?","choices":["So it does not hide your face and voice","So you can read faster","So no one sees your writing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where does your voice need to go?","explain":"A low page keeps your face and voice clear for the listeners."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Your story has {q} question marks and {e} exclamation marks. At how many of these marks does your voice change its tune?","answer":"q+e","hint":"Both a question mark and an exclamation mark change how your voice sounds.","explain":"{q} question marks and {e} exclamation marks make {=q+e} places where your voice changes.","params":{"q":[1,4],"e":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"You typed “Icanswim.” What do you need to add?","choices":["Spaces between the words","More capital letters","Another full stop"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you see where one word ends and the next starts?","explain":"Press the space bar between the words: I can swim."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"You are reading your story to the whole class. How should you speak?","choices":["Clear and loud, so the back row hears","In a whisper","Shouting every word"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the person at the very back of the room.","explain":"Clear and loud lets the back row hear. A whisper is too quiet and shouting is too much."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Your story has {a} sentences. Each one ends with a full stop. How many times do you pause at a full stop when you read it all?","answer":"a","hint":"There is one full stop at the end of each sentence.","explain":"{a} sentences have {a} full stops, so you pause {a} times.","params":{"a":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"How does your voice go at the end of “Do you like frogs?”","choices":["Up","Down","It stays flat"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is a question you answer with yes or no.","explain":"A yes or no question goes up at the end, so the listener knows you are asking."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You typed a sentence with {w} words. How many times did you press the space bar between the words?","answer":"w-1","hint":"A space goes between two words, not before the first one or after the last.","explain":"{w} words have {=w-1} gaps between them, so you press the space bar {=w-1} times.","params":{"w":[3,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["When you share your writing, you read it out to other people. Stand up tall and hold the page low, so it does not hide your face. Be clear and loud, so the back row can hear you. Do not shout.","The marks in your writing tell you how to read it. Pause at a full stop. At a question mark, like Can I come too?, your voice goes up. At an exclamation mark, say it with a strong voice.","Look up at the people now and then. Read at a steady speed, not too fast. If you trip on a word, stop, go back and read it again.","To publish means to make a best copy for readers. You plan, draft and edit first. Then you can type it. Hold Shift to make a capital letter. Press the space bar between words. Press Backspace to take away a letter."],"adult":["This topic follows the national curriculum for England. In Year 1, pupils read their writing aloud clearly enough to be heard by peers and the teacher. In Year 2 they use the right intonation to make the meaning clear. In Years 3 and 4 they read to a group or the class and control tone and volume. In Years 5 and 6 they perform their own compositions with intonation, volume and movement. The US Common Core adds digital tools to produce and publish writing, and keyboarding skills from Grade 3 onward.","Useful terms: audience, volume, intonation, tone, pace, punctuation, draft, edit, final copy, publish, and keyboarding words such as Shift key, space bar and Backspace. Punctuation is a reading map: a full stop means pause, and a question mark or exclamation mark changes the tune. A yes or no question usually rises at the end. A question that starts with what or where often falls.","Common mistakes: reading to the page instead of the room, so the voice drops; racing past full stops; shouting instead of projecting; a flat voice that hides questions and jokes; and publishing a first draft before it is edited. When typing, children often press a letter harder for a capital, run words together, or forget the final full stop. Be careful to praise a clear voice and a fixed slip, not just a neat page.","In the lab your child turns a voice dial until all four rows hear, without the front row covering their ears. They tap the three pauses in a short beach story, match three sentences to their tune, press the key that fixes four typed lines, and put plan, draft, edit, publish and share in order."]},"familyTip":"At bedtime, swap roles: your child reads you something they wrote. Sit at the far end of the room and give a thumbs up when you can hear every word.","keyIdea":"Writing is for readers and listeners. When you read your own writing aloud, be clear and loud enough for the back row, pause at full stops, question marks and exclamation marks, and let your voice show the meaning. To publish, you make a neat final copy after you edit, often by typing it: Shift for capital letters, the space bar between words, Backspace to delete.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Helps your listeners or makes it harder? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["👂 Helps listeners","🙉 Makes it harder"],"items":[["Speak clear and loud",0],["Pause at full stops",0],["Look up now and then",0],["Voice goes up for a yes or no question",0],["Mumble into the page",1],["Rush to the end",1],["Shout every word",1],["Hide your face with the page",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagpublish","caption":"Flagship lab: turn your voice up so the back row hears, pause at the marks, give each sentence its tune, fix typed lines with the right key, then publish.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_AHAFw-atka":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"You compare the speed, food and habitat of four big cats. Which device fits best?","choices":["A table","A numbered list","One long paragraph"],"answer":0,"hint":"Are you comparing several things on the same features?","explain":"A table lines up the same features for all four cats, so the reader can compare down a column."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why do information texts use sub-headings?","choices":["So readers can find the section they need","To make the text longer","Because every page must have them"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about how people use a guide: do they read every word?","explain":"Readers scan. Sub-headings name each section, so they can jump to the part they need."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which content should stay as a paragraph?","choices":["An explanation of why volcanoes erupt","A list of things to bring on a trip","The steps to tie a knot"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for ideas that are joined by because and so.","explain":"An explanation links causes and effects with because and so. Bullets would break those links."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A table about pets has no column headers. What goes wrong?","choices":["The reader cannot tell what each column means","Nothing, the facts are still there","The rows are in the wrong order"],"answer":0,"hint":"How does a reader know what a column of facts is about?","explain":"Column headers name what each column holds. Without them the reader has to guess what the facts mean."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A writer puts these as bullet points: mix the flour, bake for 20 minutes, grease the tin. What is the slip?","choices":["They are steps in order, so they should be numbered","Bullets should be full paragraphs","They need a table"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the reader need to do these in a set order?","explain":"Recipe steps happen in order, so a numbered list fits. Here the tin must be greased before baking, and numbers make that clear."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A safety poster lists things to do and things not to do. Which layout helps the reader most?","choices":["Two columns headed Do and Don't","One paragraph with every tip","A numbered list of all the tips"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which layout lets the reader see the two sets side by side?","explain":"Two headed columns keep the sets apart and side by side, so the reader can see at once what to do and what to avoid."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A table compares {a} rivers on length, source and mouth. It also has one header row. How many rows does the table have in all?","answer":"a+1","hint":"One row for each river, plus the header row across the top.","explain":"{a} river rows plus 1 header row is {=a+1} rows. The header row names the columns: length, source and mouth.","params":{"a":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which is a well-written cell in a table about planets, under the column Moons?","choices":["2","Mars has two small moons called Phobos and Deimos.","Moons"],"answer":0,"hint":"Table cells hold short notes. The headers already say what the cell is about.","explain":"The row says Mars and the column says Moons, so the cell only needs 2. Full sentences make tables slow to scan."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"A guide has {n} sections, each with a sub-heading. A reader wants the last section. Without sub-headings they read every section to find it. How many sections do they read?","answer":"n","hint":"With no labels the reader has to check each section in turn.","explain":"They check all {=n} sections. A sub-heading on each one lets them jump straight to the last section instead.","params":{"n":[3,8]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"You are writing how to make a bird feeder. The steps must be done in order. Which device fits?","choices":["A numbered list","Bullet points","A table"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ask: does the order of the points matter to the reader?","explain":"The order matters, so number the steps. Bullet points suggest the items could come in any order."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A table compares {a} animals on {b} features. Each animal has a row and each feature has a column. How many cells hold facts, not counting the headers?","answer":"a*b","hint":"Each animal row has one fact cell for every feature column.","explain":"{a} rows times {b} columns gives {=a*b} fact cells. Each fact sits where its row and its column meet.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which is the best sub-heading for a section about where owls nest?","choices":["Nesting places","Owls are birds that hunt mostly at night","Other information"],"answer":0,"hint":"A sub-heading is a short label that names what this section is about.","explain":"Nesting places names the section in two words. The long sentence is not a label, and Other information does not tell the reader anything."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A leaflet has one main heading and {n} sections. Each section gets its own sub-heading. How many headings and sub-headings are there in all?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Count one sub-heading per section, then add the main heading.","explain":"{n} sub-headings plus 1 main heading makes {=n+1}. The heading names the topic and each sub-heading names one section.","params":{"n":[2,7]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Readers of a guide or a leaflet rarely read every word. They scan for the part they need. Layout devices help them do that. A heading tells them the topic. Sub-headings split the text into sections, and each one names its section in a few words, like Food or Winter sleep.","Pick the device that fits the content. Use bullet points for separate items in no set order, such as a kit list. Use a numbered list for steps that must happen in order. If you swap two steps, the reader goes wrong, so number them.","Use a table when you compare several things on the same features. Row headers go down the side and column headers go across the top. Each fact sits where its row and column meet, written as a short note. Columns put two sets of points side by side, such as Do and Don't.","Some writing should stay as a paragraph. If you explain why something happens, your ideas are joined by because and so. Bullets would break those links. Good layout is not decoration. Each device has a job: helping the reader find, follow or compare."],"adult":["This is the Year 6 writing objective in England's national curriculum. Pupils use organisational and presentational devices to structure text and guide the reader. English Appendix 2 lists layout devices for Year 6: headings, sub-headings, columns, bullets and tables. In the US, Common Core W.4.2a and W.5.2a ask pupils to group related information in sections and include formatting such as headings when it aids understanding.","Key terms: layout device, organisational device, presentational device, heading, sub-heading, bullet point, numbered list, table, row header, column header, cell and column. The test for any device is what the reader needs to do: find a section, follow steps, scan items or compare features.","Common mistakes: sub-headings that are whole sentences or too vague to help, such as More facts. Bullets used for steps where order matters. Tables with no headers, or with full sentences crammed into cells. A careful writer also avoids bullet-pointing an explanation, which strips out the because and so that carry the reasoning.","In the lab your learner drags sub-headings onto three sections of a hedgehog guide, with a vague label and a full sentence as decoys. They sort five pieces of content into bullets, numbers, a table or a paragraph, and file six facts into a table using its headers. Then they swipe tips into Do and Don't columns and fix a leaflet with three layout slips."]},"familyTip":"Pick up a leaflet, a recipe or a museum guide together. Ask your learner to point out each layout device and say what job it does for the reader. Then ask them to find one place where the layout could work better, and how they would change it.","keyIdea":"Layout devices help a reader find their way around an information text. A heading names the whole topic and a sub-heading names one section in a few words. Bullet points list separate items, a numbered list shows steps in order, a table compares several things on the same features, and columns put two sets of points side by side. Ideas linked by because and so still belong in a paragraph.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which layout device fits each piece of content? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Bullet points","Numbered list","Table"],"items":[["What to pack: hat, water, map, snack",0],["Ways to save energy at home",0],["How to plant a seed, step by step",1],["Steps to make a paper boat",1],["Five planets compared on size and moons",2],["Opening times of three museums by day",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaglayout","caption":"Flagship lab: place sub-headings, pick the right device, build a table from its headers, sort tips into columns, and fix a leaflet's layout slips.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_lIs10UMkPG":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which word is the best start for a recount?","choices":["First","Finally","Dear"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which time word comes at the start?","explain":"First starts a recount. Finally comes at the end. Dear starts a letter."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Where does Love from Ben go in a letter?","choices":["At the end","At the top","In the middle"],"answer":0,"hint":"It tells who wrote the letter.","explain":"Love from and your name go at the end. Dear and a name go at the top."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which is a bossy verb to start a step?","choices":["Mix","Mixed","Yummy"],"answer":0,"hint":"A bossy verb tells you what to do now.","explain":"Mix tells you what to do, so it can start a step. Mixed is in the past."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"You get stuck halfway down the page. What should you do?","choices":["Read back my last line and look at my plan","Rub it all out","Stop and never finish"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one keeps the lines you already wrote?","explain":"Read back your last line and look at your plan. It shows what comes next, and you keep your work."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"You want to tell Grandad about your sports day and say hello. What do you write?","choices":["A letter to Grandad","Instructions for a race","A list of shoes"],"answer":0,"hint":"You are talking to one person.","explain":"Talking to one person is a letter: Dear Grandad, your news, then Love from and your name."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Instructions have {n} steps. You have done 1 step. How many steps are left?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Take the 1 step you did away from {n}.","explain":"{n} take away 1 is {=n-1}, so {=n-1} steps are left. Do them in order.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"You write {n} lines today and {n} lines tomorrow. How many lines is that?","answer":"n+n","hint":"Add today's lines and tomorrow's lines.","explain":"{n} and {n} more is {=n+n} lines. A bit each day builds stamina.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which recount sentence is in the right tense?","choices":["After that, we ate lunch.","After that, we will eat lunch.","Eat your lunch."],"answer":0,"hint":"A recount tells what already happened.","explain":"We ate is in the past, so it fits a recount. We will eat has not happened yet."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"You want to tell how to wash a bike. What do you write?","choices":["Instructions with numbered steps","A recount of a bike ride","A letter to the bike"],"answer":0,"hint":"The job is how to do something. Which kind of writing tells how to?","explain":"How to do something means instructions: numbered steps that start with bossy verbs, like Fill the bucket."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Your recount plan has {n} pictures. You write one sentence for each picture. How many sentences will you write?","answer":"n","hint":"One sentence for each picture. Count the pictures.","explain":"One sentence for each of the {n} pictures makes {n} sentences. A plan tells you how much to write.","params":{"n":[3,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which sentence fits a recount of a trip to the farm?","choices":["We fed the lambs.","We will feed the lambs.","Feed the lambs."],"answer":0,"hint":"A recount tells what already happened.","explain":"We fed the lambs is in the past, so it fits a recount. Feed the lambs is a bossy verb for instructions."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You wrote {a} lines, then got stuck. You read back and kept going for {b} more lines. How many lines in all?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the lines before you got stuck to the lines after.","explain":"{a} lines and {b} more lines is {=a+b} lines. Reading back kept your first {a} lines safe.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[2,4]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Pick the kind of writing for the job. How do you make a cake? Write instructions. What did you do at the zoo? Write a recount. Want to say thank you to Gran? Write a letter.","Instructions are steps, 1, 2, 3. Each step starts with a bossy verb, like Fill, Push or Mix. Do the steps in order, or it goes wrong.","A recount tells what you did. It is in the past: we went, we saw, we ate. Time words help: First, Next, After that, Finally. A letter starts with Dear Gran. The news goes in the middle. It ends with Love from and your name.","Long writing gets hard. Plan it first with pictures. If you get stuck, read back your last line. Then look at your plan to see what comes next."],"adult":["This matches Year 2 writing composition in the national curriculum for England. Pupils write about real events and their own experiences, and write for different purposes. They plan, say sentences aloud first, and re-read to check that the writing makes sense. In the US, CCSS W.1.3 asks first graders to recount two or more events in order, use temporal words to show the order and give a sense of closure. W.1.2 and W.2.2 cover informative and how-to writing.","Key terms: purpose (the job the writing does). Recount: a non-fiction retelling of real events in time order. Instructions: a procedural text with a goal, what you need and numbered steps. Imperative or command verbs are called bossy verbs in many classrooms. Temporal connectives, or time words, are first, next, after that and finally. Recounts use the past tense (went, saw, ate). A letter has a greeting (Dear …) and a sign-off (Love from …).","Common mistakes: slipping from the past tense into the future (we will see the lions) inside a recount. Putting Finally or First in the wrong place. Writing steps out of order, or as I-statements instead of commands. Putting the sign-off at the top of a letter. With stamina, the usual slip is rubbing out a whole page when stuck, which throws away good work. Stamina grows from a short plan of four pictures or key words, saying each sentence first, and a habit for getting unstuck: read back the last line, look at the plan, write the next bit. Praise the finished page more than neat letters.","In the lab your child matches writing jobs to forms. They order seed-planting steps and see the pot go wrong when a step comes too soon. They pick past-tense sentences with the right time words for a zoo recount, lay out a thank-you letter, and fill a page past two stuck moments. Every drag has a big button twin."]},"familyTip":"After a day out, draw four quick pictures together of what happened, in order. Then your child writes one sentence for each, starting with First, Next, After that and Finally. If they get stuck, ask them to read back the last line and point to the next picture.","keyIdea":"Different jobs need different kinds of writing. Instructions tell how to do something, in numbered steps that start with a bossy verb such as Fill or Mix. A recount tells what happened, in time order, with time words such as First, Next, After that and Finally, and verbs in the past tense, such as went and saw. A letter starts with Dear and a name and ends with Love from and your name. To keep writing for longer, plan first, then read back your last line and look at your plan when you get stuck.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Recount or instructions? Drag each sentence, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🕰️ Recount: what happened","🔢 Instructions: how to"],"items":[["We went to the park.",0],["Stir the paint.",1],["Next, we fed the ducks.",0],["Cut the paper in half.",1],["Finally, we walked home.",0],["Put on your coat.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagstam","caption":"Flagship lab: match writing jobs to instructions, recount or letter, put steps in order, write a recount, lay out a letter and keep going when stuck.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_YNe6siFTFq":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which pair of words rhymes?","choices":["night and light","sun and sock","fish and frog"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for two words that end the same way.","explain":"Night and light end with the same ite sound. The other pairs only start the same."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Finish the couplet: “I have a little boat. / I hope that it will …”","choices":["float","sink","swim"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word ends like boat?","explain":"Boat and float rhyme, so the two lines make a couplet."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A poem goes: “I can see a red bus. I can see a blue car.” Which line keeps the pattern?","choices":["I can see a green van.","The van is green.","I can see a red bus."],"answer":0,"hint":"Keep the start the same, but say something new.","explain":"“I can see a green van” keeps the start I can see and adds a new thing."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"How should you read “BANG! went the drum”?","choices":["Loudly","In a whisper","Very slowly"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the big letters and the !.","explain":"BANG in big letters with an ! tells you to read it loudly."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Why do “bus” and “bell” not rhyme?","choices":["They only start the same","They are too short","One is a thing you ride"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where do rhyming words match: at the start or at the end?","explain":"Rhymes match at the end. Bus and bell only share the b at the start."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which line has the same claps as “Hot red sun” (3 claps)?","choices":["Cold white snow","Snow","Lots of white snow on the hill"],"answer":0,"hint":"Clap each line and count.","explain":"Cold white snow is 3 claps, the same as hot red sun."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Sam’s pattern poem has {a} lines. He adds {b} more lines that start “I can hear”. How many lines now?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Start with the lines he has and add the new ones.","explain":"{a} lines and {b} more make {=a+b} lines in his poem.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A couplet ends with a pair of rhyming words. Zoe writes {a} couplets. How many rhyming words are at the line ends?","answer":"2*a","hint":"Each couplet ends with a pair of rhyming words.","explain":"{a} couplets each end with 2 rhyming words, so {=2*a} words.","params":{"a":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Which two words rhyme even though they are spelt in a different way?","choices":["blue and shoe","blue and black","shoe and ship"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say them. Listen, do not just look.","explain":"Blue and shoe both end with the oo sound, so they rhyme. Rhyme is about sound, not spelling."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which word rhymes with “tree”?","choices":["bee","tray","leaf"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say the words and listen to the end sound.","explain":"Tree and bee end with the same ee sound. Tray starts like tree, but it ends in a different way."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Mia writes {a} rhyming couplets. A couplet is 2 lines. How many lines does she write?","answer":"2*a","hint":"Each couplet has 2 lines. Count 2 for each one.","explain":"{a} couplets with 2 lines each make {=2*a} lines.","params":{"a":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"How many claps are in “dinosaur”?","choices":["3","1","8"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say it slowly and clap each beat: di … no … saur.","explain":"Di-no-saur has 3 beats, so 3 claps. Clap the beats, not the letters."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A poem has {a} lines. Each line has {b} claps. How many claps in the whole poem?","answer":"a*b","hint":"Add the claps for each line. Every line has the same number.","explain":"{a} lines with {b} claps each make {=a*b} claps in the poem.","params":{"a":[2,4],"b":[3,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A poem plays with the sound of words. Say cat, mat, hat. They end the same. That is a rhyme. Two lines that end in a rhyme make a couplet: I see a big red bus. It makes a lot of fuss.","Words that start the same do not rhyme. Bus and bell both start with b, but the ends are not the same. Listen to the end of the word, not the start. High and sky rhyme, but they are spelt in different ways.","Poems have a beat. Clap once for each beat as you say a line. Hop, hop, hop is 3 claps. A long word can get more than one clap: el-e-phants is 3. Lines that go together sound best with the same claps.","Some poems use a pattern. Each line starts the same, like I can hear, and ends in a new way: the bees buzz, the rain drips. Then say your poem out loud. Shh means soft. Big letters mean loud. Creeps slowly means slow."],"adult":["In England, Year 2 pupils write poetry and learn poems by heart, reciting them with appropriate intonation so the meaning is clear (National Curriculum, KS1). The US standards ask the same age to describe how words and phrases, such as regular beats, alliteration, rhymes and repeated lines, supply rhythm and meaning in a poem or song (CCSS RL.2.4).","Three tools carry most early poems. Rhyme is a match of the final vowel sound and everything after it, the rime (c-at, m-at); a rhyming couplet is two lines that end in a rhyme. Rhythm is the pattern of beats, and at this age a clap per syllable is the working measure. Repetition, such as a repeated opening, gives a pattern poem its shape and helps children remember it.","Common mistakes: treating words that start alike as rhymes (bus and bell are alliteration), judging rhyme by spelling rather than sound (high and sky rhyme; cough and dough do not), giving a long word a single clap, ending a pattern by copying a whole earlier line, and reciting every line in the same flat voice.","In the lab your child finishes three couplets with a rhyme, matches the claps of two lines, adds lines to an I can hear pattern poem, and picks a loud, soft, fast or slow voice from clues in the words. Every drag has a big button twin."]},"familyTip":"Make an I can hear poem together on a walk or at the window. Each of you adds a line that starts I can hear. Then clap the beats of one line, and read the whole poem once in a whisper and once in a big voice.","keyIdea":"A poem is words that sound good together. Rhyming words end with the same sound, like bus and fuss, or high and sky. Two lines that rhyme make a couplet. Clap the beats in a line: el-e-phants is 3 claps, not 1. A pattern poem repeats the start of each line and changes the end. When you say a poem aloud, let the words tell your voice to be loud, soft, fast or slow.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does it rhyme with cat? Drag each word to a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Rhymes with cat","Does not rhyme"],"items":[["hat",0],["mat",0],["flat",0],["cap",1],["cot",1],["dog",1],["bat",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcouplet","caption":"Flagship lab: finish couplets with a rhyme, match the claps of two lines, keep a pattern poem going, and pick the voice that fits the words.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_v5DyOEpbbr":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which opening suits a letter to the town council?","choices":["Dear Sir or Madam,","Hiya council!","Once upon a time,"],"answer":0,"hint":"The council is an official audience you do not know.","explain":"A formal audience needs a formal opening: Dear Sir or Madam."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Your reader is 6. Which sentence suits them best?","choices":["Bees carry pollen from flower to flower.","Bees facilitate pollen transfer between flowers.","Bees do pollen stuff lol."],"answer":0,"hint":"Keep the facts, but use easy words.","explain":"Easy words and the same facts. “Facilitate” is too hard, and “lol” drops the facts."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What is the purpose of a joke book?","choices":["To entertain","To inform","To persuade"],"answer":0,"hint":"What should the reader feel?","explain":"Jokes are there to make the reader enjoy it and laugh, so the purpose is to entertain."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which line does NOT fit a letter asking the head for a bike rack?","choices":["Step 1: buy a spanner.","Many of us cycle to school.","Please could we have one by summer?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which line belongs to a different form?","explain":"Numbered steps belong in instructions. The letter's purpose is to persuade."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A review of a new game should include…","choices":["Your opinion and reasons","Only facts about the box","Numbered steps"],"answer":0,"hint":"What makes a review different from a report?","explain":"A review tells the reader what you thought and why, often with a rating."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A tone dial goes from 0 (very chatty) to 4 (very formal). It is on {d}. The council needs 4. How many clicks towards formal?","answer":"4-d","hint":"Each click moves the dial up by one. Count on from {d} until you reach 4.","explain":"From {d} up to 4 is {=4-d} clicks towards formal.","params":{"d":[0,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Is formal writing always better?","choices":["No, it depends on the audience","Yes, always","Only in stories"],"answer":0,"hint":"How would a very formal text to your best friend sound?","explain":"Formal suits officials and strangers. To a friend, chatty fits better. Match the tone to the reader."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Mia is asked for the purpose of her bike-rack letter. She says “It is about bikes.” What did she give?","choices":["The topic, not the purpose","The right purpose","The form"],"answer":0,"hint":"Purpose is why you write, not what it is about.","explain":"Bikes are the topic. The purpose is to persuade the head to say yes."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"A sentence for a 6-year-old has {w} words. You swap {n} hard words for easy ones. How many words is it now?","answer":"w","hint":"A swap takes one word out and puts one word in.","explain":"Each swap is one word for one word, so it still has {w} words. Only the hard words changed.","params":{"w":[6,10],"n":[1,3]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"You write to ask the head teacher for a longer lunch break. What is your purpose?","choices":["To persuade","To entertain","To instruct"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do you want the head to do after reading it?","explain":"You want the head to agree and act, so the purpose is to persuade."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Your instructions have {n} steps. Each step starts with one bossy verb. How many bossy verbs are there?","answer":"n","hint":"One bossy verb, like fold or press, at the start of each step.","explain":"{n} steps with one imperative verb each gives {n} bossy verbs.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A report has {h} headings with {f} facts under each. How many facts is that?","answer":"h*f","hint":"Each heading has the same number of facts. Multiply.","explain":"{h} headings times {f} facts is {=h*f} facts.","params":{"h":[2,4],"f":[2,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which form fits “How to look after a goldfish”?","choices":["Instructions","A story","A review"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the reader need steps to follow?","explain":"The reader needs to know what to do, in order, so instructions with numbered steps fit best."}],"explain":{"kid":["Writers make choices before they start. They ask two questions. Who is my audience, the person who will read this? And what is my purpose? The four big purposes are to inform (give facts), persuade (get someone to agree or act), entertain (make them enjoy it) and instruct (tell them how to do something).","The purpose helps you pick the form, the kind of text. Want the head teacher to say yes to a bike rack? Write a letter. Sharing facts about birds? A report, with headings. Showing how to fold a paper plane? Instructions, with numbered steps and bossy verbs like fold and press. Saying if a film is good? A review, with your opinion and a rating. Want to make people laugh? Try a story.","The audience sets the tone. Tone is how the writing sounds, from chatty to very formal. A note to your best friend can say “Hi! Can we use the hall on Friday?” A letter to the town council needs “Dear Sir or Madam, I am writing to ask whether we may use the hall on Friday.” Same message, different reader, different tone.","The audience also changes your words. For a 6-year-old, “Precipitation descends from cumulonimbus clouds” becomes “Rain falls from huge storm clouds.” The facts stay. When you check your work, look for lines that do not fit, like “lol” in a letter to the head, or “Once upon a time” in a letter asking for something."],"adult":["This matches the upper Key Stage 2 writing goals in the English national curriculum. In Years 5 and 6, pupils should plan by identifying the audience for and purpose of the writing and by selecting the appropriate form. They should also select vocabulary and grammar to suit, and recognise how formal speech and writing differ from informal. In the US, Common Core W.4.4 and W.5.4 ask for writing whose development and organisation suit the task, purpose and audience. L.4.3c and L.5.3 cover choosing formal or informal English by context.","A few terms help. Purpose is why the text exists; teachers often use the four broad purposes inform, persuade, entertain and instruct. Form, or text type, is the shape that serves it: letter, report, instructions, review, story. Each has features children can name, such as numbered steps and imperative verbs in instructions. Tone is the attitude the text sounds like. Register, or level of formality, is a big part of tone. Lesson 185 practises the formal and informal word swaps; this lesson adds purpose, form and writing for a younger reader.","Common mistakes to watch for: naming the topic when asked for the purpose (it is about bikes, rather than it is to persuade); picking a form by habit, so everything becomes a story; opening a letter to an official with Hiya or text speak; thinking formal is always best, so a note to a friend sounds stiff; making text for a younger reader easier by dropping the facts instead of swapping hard words; and mixing forms in one piece, such as a Step 1 list inside a persuasive letter.","In the lab your child names the purpose of four writing tasks and drags five tasks onto the form that fits. They slide a tone dial until one message suits a best friend, a teacher and the town council. They swap hard words so two science sentences work for a 6-year-old, and find three lines in a letter to the head that break its tone or form. The Challenge mixes all of these, with a hint for each wrong choice."]},"familyTip":"Pick one small piece of news, like a lost cat or a school fair. Write it three ways together: a text to a friend, a poster for the village hall and a letter to the local paper. Then read them aloud and ask: what changed, and why did it need to?","keyIdea":"Before writing, ask who the audience is and what the purpose is: to inform, persuade, entertain or instruct. The purpose points to a form (a letter, report, set of instructions, review or story), and the audience sets the tone and the vocabulary. The same message can be written chatty for a friend, polite for a teacher and very formal for a council, and the same facts can be told in easier words for a younger reader. A line in the wrong tone or the wrong form makes the whole piece work less well.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Chatty or formal? Drag each opening, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Chatty: for a friend","Formal: for the council"],"items":[["Hiya!",0],["Dear Sir or Madam,",1],["Guess what?",0],["I am writing to ask",1],["Can't wait!",0],["Yours faithfully",1],["See ya",0],["Thank you for reading",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagaud","caption":"Flagship lab: name the purpose, pick the form, set a tone dial for three readers, simplify for a 6-year-old and spot lines that do not fit.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_9lN0SpKlEH":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"In a UK formal letter, where does your own address go?","choices":["Top right, with the date below","At the bottom, after your name","Under the salutation"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is the first thing on the page, on the opposite side to the reader's address.","explain":"Your address goes top right, with the date beneath it. The reader's address goes on the left."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which is the best set of notes for a talk?","choices":["• Hook: a question • Point 1: cost • End: ask for questions","A full page of sentences to read out","One long paragraph with no breaks"],"answer":0,"hint":"You need to glance at notes and look up at the audience.","explain":"Short bullet prompts let you glance down and keep eye contact. A full page tempts you to read it out."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which split into lines works best for a poem?","choices":["The rain keeps drumming / on the roof of the bus","The rain keeps drumming on the / roof of the bus","The rain keeps / drumming on the roof of / the bus"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the last word of each line. Is it strong or small?","explain":"Ending on \"drumming\" and \"bus\" gives each line a strong last word. Ending on \"the\" or \"of\" leaves lines hanging."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"You change a story extract into a script. What do you take out?","choices":["Speech marks and \"he said\"","The speaker names","The stage directions"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which features belong to stories, not scripts?","explain":"Scripts drop speech marks and reporting clauses like \"he said\"; the speaker name shows who talks."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A talk has {p} main points. Each needs 1 bullet, and you add 1 bullet for the hook and 1 for the ending. How many bullets?","answer":"p+2","hint":"Count one bullet per main point, then add the hook and the ending.","explain":"{p} point bullets plus a hook and an ending make {=p+2} bullets.","params":{"p":[2,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A poem has {s} stanzas of {k} lines. You add one more stanza of {k} lines. How many lines now?","answer":"s*k+k","hint":"Find the lines in {s} stanzas first, then add {k} more for the new stanza.","explain":"{s} stanzas of {k} lines is {=s*k} lines. One more stanza adds {k}, making {=s*k+k}.","params":{"s":[2,4],"k":[3,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A letter starts \"Dear Mr Khan\". Which sign-off matches?","choices":["Yours sincerely","Yours faithfully","Love from"],"answer":0,"hint":"You know the reader's name. Which sign-off goes with a named greeting?","explain":"A named greeting, \"Dear Mr Khan\", pairs with \"Yours sincerely\" in a UK formal letter."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which sentence suits the register of a formal letter of complaint?","choices":["I am writing to complain about the late buses on Route 12.","OMG the buses are sooo late lol","Hiya, buses = rubbish!!"],"answer":0,"hint":"Formal register means full sentences and no slang.","explain":"A formal letter uses full sentences, standard English and no slang, so the first line fits."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A letter begins \"Dear Sir or Madam\". Which sign-off matches?","choices":["Yours faithfully","Yours sincerely","Best wishes, mate"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do you know the reader's name? Which sign-off goes with an unknown name?","explain":"With \"Dear Sir or Madam\" the name is not known, so a UK formal letter ends \"Yours faithfully\"."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A script has {a} lines for Maya and {b} lines for Tom. How many speaker names will appear on the left?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Every line of a script starts with a speaker name. Add Maya's {a} lines and Tom's {b}.","explain":"Each line starts with its speaker's name, so {a} + {b} = {=a+b} names appear.","params":{"a":[3,8],"b":[2,7]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A poem has {s} stanzas, each with {n} lines. How many lines is that?","answer":"s*n","hint":"A stanza is a group of lines. Multiply the stanzas by the lines in each one.","explain":"{s} stanzas of {n} lines make {=s*n} lines in all.","params":{"s":[2,5],"n":[3,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which line is written as a script?","choices":["TOM: (quietly) I think so.","\"I think so,\" Tom said quietly.","Tom said quietly that he thought so."],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a speaker name, a colon and brackets.","explain":"A script line starts with the speaker name and a colon, and puts how it is said in brackets."}],"explain":{"kid":["A genre or form is a kind of writing: a story, a script, a poem, a letter, notes for a talk. Each form has conventions. These are the rules of layout and style that readers expect. You can often name the form from its shape before you read a word.","A formal letter has a set layout. Your address and the date go at the top right. The reader's name and address go on the left. Then comes the salutation, the greeting. If you write \"Dear Sir or Madam\", you sign off \"Yours faithfully\". If you use their name, as in \"Dear Ms Patel\", you sign off \"Yours sincerely\". The register is formal: full sentences and no slang.","A script shows speech in a different way from a story. Each line starts with the speaker's name and a colon. There are no speech marks and no \"he said\". How a line is spoken, and what people do, goes in brackets as stage directions.","Notes for a talk are short bullet points you glance at, so you can look up at your audience. A poem is set out in lines. Where each line ends is called lineation. A line usually ends best on a strong word, like a noun or a verb, not on \"the\" or \"of\"."],"adult":["The Key Stage 3 English programme of study asks pupils to write for a wide range of purposes and audiences. This includes stories, scripts and poetry, notes and talks, and personal and formal letters. They should also draw on what they read to choose the right form and register. In the US, Common Core W.6.4 to W.8.4 ask for writing whose organisation and style suit the task, purpose and audience. Lesson 470 covers choosing a form and tone for a purpose; this lesson goes one step further into the conventions of each form.","Useful terms: form or genre is the kind of text. Conventions are the layout and language features readers expect. Register is the level of formality. Salutation and sign-off are the greeting and closing of a letter. Stage directions are the bracketed instructions in a script, and lineation is how a poem is divided into lines, which may be grouped into stanzas. UK letters pair \"Dear Sir or Madam\" with \"Yours faithfully\" and a named greeting with \"Yours sincerely\"; US letters usually end with \"Sincerely\" in both cases.","Common mistakes to watch for: keeping speech marks and \"he said\" in a script; pairing \"Dear Sir or Madam\" with \"Yours sincerely\"; writing out a whole talk word for word and reading it aloud; and chopping prose into random lines and calling it a poem. A careful writer checks each convention against a model text before handing work in.","In the lab, learners name four fragments from their conventions and build a formal letter in the right order. They match salutations to sign-offs, then turn a prose extract into a script one change at a time. Last, they break a sentence into four lines of poetry that end on strong words. The Challenge mixes these, with feedback on each wrong answer."]},"keyIdea":"Each form of writing has conventions: the layout and features its readers expect. A script uses speaker names, colons and stage directions in brackets, with no speech marks. A formal letter follows a set layout, from the addresses and date to a salutation, paragraphs and a sign-off that matches the greeting. Notes for a talk are short prompts, not full sentences. A poem uses lineation, so where each line ends matters. Choosing the right form, register and conventions makes a piece work for its purpose and audience.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which form is the feature from? Drag each one into a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Script","Formal letter"],"items":[["Speaker names and colons",0],["Stage directions in brackets",0],["No speech marks",0],["Cast list at the start",0],["Your address top right",1],["Dear Sir or Madam,",1],["Yours faithfully",1],["The date",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaggenre","caption":"Flagship lab: name forms from their conventions, build a formal letter, match sign-offs, turn prose into a script and break a sentence into lines of poetry.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Qxyikkkzam":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is a rebuttal?","choices":["An answer that shows why the counterclaim does not win","A second claim on a new topic","A summary at the end"],"answer":0,"hint":"It comes straight after the counterclaim and replies to it.","explain":"A rebuttal answers the counterclaim with a reason, so your claim still stands."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which is the best counterclaim for 'Pupils should be allowed phones at break'?","choices":["Some teachers worry phones lead to online bullying at break.","Anyone who disagrees is old-fashioned.","There is no reason to disagree."],"answer":0,"hint":"A fair counterclaim gives the other side's best reason without insults.","explain":"The first states a real worry fairly. Insults and 'no other side' make the writer look less trustworthy."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which sentence is in a formal style?","choices":["This change would benefit many pupils.","This is a total no-brainer.","Honestly, it's kinda obvious, right?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Formal writing avoids slang, chatty words and talking to the reader like a friend.","explain":"The first uses standard, precise words. The others are chatty or use slang."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Sam's conclusion says: 'Also, the canteen should sell pizza.' What is wrong?","choices":["It adds a new point instead of summing up","It is too formal","It repeats the claim"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the job of a concluding statement.","explain":"A conclusion sums up the claim and the reasons. A new point belongs earlier, or not at all."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which word shows a result: 'Pupils are tired. ___, school should start later.'","choices":["Therefore","However","For example"],"answer":0,"hint":"You need a word meaning 'so, because of this'.","explain":"Therefore draws a result from the reason before it. However turns, and for example gives evidence."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"You plan {n} reasons, each with {k} pieces of evidence. How many pieces of evidence will you use in total?","answer":"n*k","hint":"Each reason has the same number of pieces of evidence. Multiply.","explain":"{n} × {k} = {=n*k} pieces of evidence.","params":{"n":[2,4],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Your argument has an introduction, {n} reasons, one counterclaim and a conclusion, each in its own paragraph. You have written {k}. How many are left?","answer":"n+3-k","hint":"First count all the paragraphs: 1 + reasons + 1 + 1. Then take away what is done.","explain":"There are {=n+3} paragraphs in total, and {=n+3} − {k} = {=n+3-k} are left.","params":{"n":[2,4],"k":[1,2]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Mia writes: 'Some say homework helps. That is a good point, so maybe homework is fine.' What should she do instead?","choices":["Answer the counterclaim with a reason her claim still wins","Delete the counterclaim","Insult people who like homework"],"answer":0,"hint":"Naming the other side is good. What should come straight after it?","explain":"She gave in. A rebuttal accepts the worry, then explains why her claim is still stronger."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which sentence is an arguable claim?","choices":["Young children should have no more than one hour of screen time a day.","Many children use tablets.","Screens are made of glass."],"answer":0,"hint":"A claim is a view that someone could reasonably disagree with.","explain":"The first sentence takes a side that people could argue about. The others are facts anyone can check."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Which is the most relevant evidence for 'Schools should serve breakfast'?","choices":["A study found pupils who ate breakfast scored higher in maths.","My brother loves toast.","Breakfast is the first meal of the day."],"answer":0,"hint":"Relevant evidence is measured or researched, and links to the reason.","explain":"The study links breakfast to learning, which supports the claim. An anecdote or a definition does not."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Your plan has an introduction, {n} reason paragraphs, one counterclaim paragraph and a conclusion. How many paragraphs is that?","answer":"n+3","hint":"Count the introduction, the counterclaim and the conclusion, then add the reasons.","explain":"1 + {n} + 1 + 1 = {=n+3} paragraphs in the whole argument.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Your teacher wants 2 pieces of evidence for each reason. You have {n} reasons. How many pieces of evidence do you need?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Each reason needs its own 2 pieces of evidence. Count 2 for each reason.","explain":"{n} reasons × 2 = {=2*n} pieces of evidence.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which linking word introduces the other side?","choices":["However","Furthermore","Therefore"],"answer":0,"hint":"You need a word that turns away from your own point.","explain":"However signals contrast, so it introduces the counterclaim. Furthermore adds and therefore concludes."}],"explain":{"kid":["Want a later bedtime? 'Because I want to' will not persuade anyone. A claim needs reasons, and each reason needs proof: a fact, a figure or what research found.","Start with your claim: what you think should happen. Give each reason its own paragraph, and back it with evidence that really fits that reason, not just a story about your cousin.","Then show the other side. Say what someone who disagrees might argue, fairly, and answer it. That answer is called a rebuttal, and it makes readers trust you more.","Glue it together with linking words: furthermore adds a point, however turns to the other side, therefore shows a result. Keep it formal, with no slang, and finish by summing up."],"adult":["This matches Common Core W.6-8.1: write arguments that back a claim with clear reasons and relevant evidence. It also fits the KS3 English aim of writing formal essays and arguments. Key terms: claim, reason, evidence, counterclaim, rebuttal, connective, formal style and concluding statement.","A good plan for ages 11 to 14 has five parts. First comes the claim. Next come two or three reasons, each with evidence. Then a counterclaim and a rebuttal. Last, a conclusion. Linking words such as however and therefore show how each part connects to the next.","Common mistakes: writing a fact instead of a claim; using one story or 'everyone knows' as evidence; leaving out the other side, or naming it and then giving in; mocking people who disagree instead of answering their reason; using slang; and adding a new point in the conclusion.","In the lab your child builds one argument, 'Schools should start at 9:30', step by step. They pick the claim, match evidence to each reason, write a fair counterclaim and rebuttal, choose linking words, make slang formal, and then put the five paragraphs in order."]},"familyTip":"Pick a real family debate, such as pocket money or screen time. Ask your child to write one claim, two reasons with a piece of evidence each, and the best argument against them, then answer it. Swap sides and see who writes the fairer counterclaim.","keyIdea":"A written argument states a claim (a view someone could disagree with), supports it with reasons and relevant evidence, acknowledges a counterclaim fairly and rebuts it, links ideas with words such as furthermore, however and therefore, keeps a formal style, and ends with a concluding statement that sums up the case.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which part of an argument is each sentence? Drag it to a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Claim","Evidence","Counterclaim","Rebuttal"],"items":[["Uniform should be optional in Year 9.",0],["A survey in our school found 7 in 10 pupils find the uniform uncomfortable.",1],["Some say uniform stops bullying about clothes.",2],["However, bullying can be tackled with clear rules instead.",3],["Phones should be locked away during lessons.",0],["Some argue phones help in emergencies.",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagargue","caption":"Flagship lab: build an argument piece by piece: claim, evidence, counterclaim and rebuttal, connectives, formal style, then order.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_BLzNxSSdWu":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Where does the question mark go? “Are you coming_” asked Sam_","choices":["Inside the closing speech mark, then a full stop after Sam","After the word Sam, at the end","Outside the closing speech mark"],"answer":0,"hint":"The question belongs to the spoken words.","explain":"It reads “Are you coming?” asked Sam. The question mark is part of the speech, so it goes inside the marks."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"In a conversation, when should you start a new paragraph?","choices":["Each time the speaker changes","Only when the scene changes","After every sentence"],"answer":0,"hint":"How does a reader keep track of who is talking?","explain":"A new speaker means a new paragraph. One speaker’s words and actions stay together."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which tag is usually the best choice for plain speech?","choices":["said","chortled","exclaimed loudly"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word lets the reader focus on the spoken words?","explain":"Said is almost invisible to readers, so the speech itself does the work. Fancy tags and adverbs pull attention away."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Who is more likely to say “Nope. Not happening.”?","choices":["A blunt, impatient teenager","A formal, polite headteacher","You cannot tell from the words"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the length of the sentences and the word choice.","explain":"Short, clipped words and no politeness fit a blunt character. Voice shows who is speaking."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A conversation has {n} turns. Each turn needs an opening and a closing speech mark. How many speech marks is that?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Every turn has one opening mark and one closing mark.","explain":"{n} turns with 2 marks each is {=2*n} speech marks.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What does an action beat do?","choices":["Shows a feeling and who is speaking through an action","Names the feeling with an adverb","Ends the conversation"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of Gran’s cup rattling on its saucer.","explain":"An action like a rattling cup shows Gran is nervous and tells us she is the one speaking, without an adverb."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which opening sets up the setting and point of view with a hook?","choices":["The attic smelled of dust, and I knew I shouldn’t be up there.","This is a story about a diary.","Once there was a boy. He was called Leo."],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one puts you in a place, inside a narrator’s head, with a question in your mind?","explain":"It gives a setting (the attic), a first-person point of view (I) and a hook (why shouldn’t they be there?)."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why is “It was all a dream” a weak ending?","choices":["It cancels everything that happened, so nothing matters","Dreams cannot be written about","It is too short to be an ending"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happens to the events of the story if none of them were real?","explain":"A dream ending wipes out the events, so the reader feels cheated. A reflective ending shows what they meant."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"Your scene has {a} lines of dialogue. {b} of them only repeat what the narrator already said, so you cut them. How many lines are left?","answer":"a-b","where":"b<a","hint":"Take away the lines that do no new work.","explain":"{a} take away {b} leaves {=a-b} lines. Every line left now shows character or moves the plot.","params":{"a":[6,12],"b":[1,4]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which line is punctuated correctly?","choices":["“Close the door,” said Dad.","“Close the door.” said Dad.","“Close the door”, said Dad."],"answer":0,"hint":"The sentence goes on after the speech, and the punctuation sits next to the spoken words.","explain":"A comma, not a full stop, comes before a tag like said Dad, and it goes inside the closing speech mark."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Two characters take turns to speak. There are {n} lines of speech, and the speaker changes every time. How many paragraphs does the dialogue need?","answer":"n","hint":"Each change of speaker starts a new paragraph.","explain":"The speaker changes on every line, so each of the {n} lines gets its own paragraph.","params":{"n":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which line best shows that Ana is nervous?","choices":["Ana twisted her sleeve. “Is it my turn?”","“Is it my turn?” Ana asked nervously.","“I am very nervous,” said Ana."],"answer":0,"hint":"Which line lets you see the feeling instead of being told it?","explain":"Twisting her sleeve is an action beat that shows nerves. The other two name the feeling."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A scene has {a} lines by Kez and {b} lines by Mr Ashby. They take turns, so each line is a new speaker. How many paragraphs is that?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Count every line, because each one is a change of speaker.","explain":"{a} lines and {b} lines make {=a+b} turns, so {=a+b} paragraphs.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which ending reflects on the story?","choices":["I still think about that night, and how Gran trusted me with the truth.","Then we went home. The end.","Suddenly I woke up. It was all a dream."],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one looks back on what the events meant?","explain":"The first ending looks back and shows what changed for the narrator. The others stop the action or cancel the story."}],"explain":{"kid":["Dialogue is what your characters say out loud. Put their exact words inside speech marks. The comma or question mark goes inside the closing mark: “Don’t open it,” Mia whispered. The tag, like Mia whispered, starts with a small letter because the sentence is not over.","Start a new line each time a new person speaks. That is how the reader keeps track of who is talking. A character’s actions stay in the same paragraph as their own words.","Show feelings, do not just name them. “I’m fine,” Gran said nervously tells us. “I’m fine.” Gran’s cup rattled on its saucer shows us. Said is a good tag because readers hardly notice it. An action beat can replace the tag and show who is speaking.","Give each character their own voice. A blunt person uses short words. A formal person uses long, polite sentences. Open with a hook that sets the scene and the point of view. End by looking back at what the events meant or how a character changed. That is stronger than a twist or a dream."],"adult":["Ages 11 to 14. This matches CCSS W.6.3 to W.8.3: write narratives that set up context and point of view, develop characters through dialogue, pacing and description, and end with a conclusion that reflects on events. It also matches the KS3 English writing aim to write imaginative stories with effective dialogue. Key terms: dialogue, speech marks (inverted commas), reporting clause or tag, action beat, voice, characterisation, reflective conclusion.","The conventions are fixed. Spoken words go inside inverted commas. Punctuation that belongs to the speech goes inside the closing mark. A comma replaces the full stop when a reporting clause follows. Each new speaker starts a new paragraph. Writers on craft, such as Browne and King, advise using said or an action beat, and letting the words and actions carry the emotion.","Common mistakes: a full stop before the tag (“Wait here.” said Mum); the comma outside the closing mark; one paragraph for a whole conversation; a fancy tag or an adverb on every line; characters who all sound alike; dialogue that only repeats what the narrator said; and endings that stop at the last action, or cancel the story with a dream. Be careful that a ‘reflective’ ending reflects on these events, not a general moral bolted on.","In the lab your child fills six punctuation gaps in two lines of speech, splits a block of dialogue into paragraphs, chooses action beats over adverbs three times, sorts six lines between a blunt and a formal speaker by voice alone, and picks the two endings that reflect."]},"familyTip":"Read a page of dialogue from a novel your child likes. Cover the tags and see if you can still tell who is speaking, then talk about how. Next, write a short argument between two very different people together, taking one character each, and set it out with speech marks and new lines.","keyIdea":"Dialogue lets readers hear characters and see who they are. Put the exact spoken words inside speech marks, with the comma, question mark or exclamation mark inside the closing mark. Start a new paragraph each time the speaker changes. Use said, or an action beat that shows the feeling, rather than adverbs that tell it. Give each character a voice of their own. End the story by reflecting on what the events meant, not just on the last thing that happened.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does the line show the feeling, or just tell it? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Shows the feeling","Tells the feeling"],"items":[["Kez kicked the bin. “Fine.”",0],["“Fine,” Kez said angrily.",1],["Leo’s voice cracked. “Thanks.”",0],["“I feel very sad,” Leo said.",1],["Gran checked the lock twice.",0],["Gran was really worried.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagdlg","caption":"Flagship lab: punctuate speech, give each speaker a new line, use action beats, hear each voice and end with reflection.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
